window.__GNAMMA_SANANDAJI84.push(...[{"id":"I064.lower.01","volume":1,"page":64,"physical_pdf_page":77,"layer":"lower","title":"whether the same quiddity is also 'the agent'","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«ليس بمحل الفاعل كما صرح به في شرح المقاصد» · «يجعل الفاعل ما به يكون الفاعل» · «بها يكون الشيء هو هو».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The commentator remarks that what is at issue is the quiddity itself, not an arbitrary attribution of agency or action to it. A definition of *agent* appeals to what makes an agent an agent; the definition of *quiddity* appeals to what makes the thing the thing it is. It does not follow that a quiddity, simply because it is a quiddity, is its own producer. Confusion can arise if the particle “by which” is given one and the same causal force wherever it occurs.","source_image":"source_images/I064.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I064.lower.02","volume":1,"page":64,"physical_pdf_page":77,"layer":"lower","title":"the 'whatness' of the essence cannot be identified with all its descriptions","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«وأقول ذلك ممنوع على هذا التقدير أيضا» · «الماهية ما به الشيء هو هو» · «المعروض للعوارض» · «تغاير المفهوم».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"“I say: that is denied even on this interpretation.” The proposed account still threatens to identify the subject described with its accidents or with the meaning of a phrase predicated of it. The question *what is it?* is not answered by supplying any description that happens to be true. Rather, the definitional content must be distinguished from subsequent concomitants, even when the same actual subject supports them all. This is why a verbal change in the proposed definition does not automatically resolve the difficulty.","source_image":"source_images/I064.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I064.lower.03","volume":1,"page":64,"physical_pdf_page":77,"layer":"lower","title":"generic, specific, and differential descriptions","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الجزء» · «الضرر واللزوم» · «الضربين من الضمير» · «خبر الذي» · «الماء ماء».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The gloss then tests the account against the familiar parts of definition: what is predicated by a genus, what is added as a differentia, and what belongs only as a property or accident. The expressions may refer to the same subject and differ in their meanings, yet still not be interchangeable in an answer to *what is it?* Repetition of an identical noun (“water is water”) supplies no new conception, whereas a suitable definition does; an accidental description, even if informative, may not answer the question in the requisite way. The last lines introduce an objection concerning the **quiddity considered without an accompanying condition**, continued on I:65.","source_image":"source_images/I064.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I064.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":64,"physical_pdf_page":77,"layer":"ruled","title":"a separate objection to describing the division as a real partition","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«(قوله قد تؤخذ الخ)» · «إنما لم يقل تنقسم الماهية إلى كذا» · «المقسم يجب أن يكون واحدا» · «هذه الاعتبارات للماهية الواحدة».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"On “It may be taken ...”: The expression is **not** simply “quiddity is divided into these species,” because the thing divided must be one in the relevant sense, whereas these are modes of taking or considering the same quiddity. A partition into different sorts of real things would be misleading: one does not first have three externally distinct quiddities which are subsequently labelled by their conditions. One rather makes different conceptual qualifications of what is one as a subject of consideration.","source_image":"source_images/I064.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I064.upper.01","volume":1,"page":64,"physical_pdf_page":77,"layer":"upper","title":"the three considerations","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«قد تؤخذ بشرط شيء» · «أي المقارنة للعوارض» · «تسمى المخلوطة» · «تخلطها مع العوارض» · «ولاحقا في وجودها».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"Quiddity may be considered **on the condition of something** (*bi-šarṭ šayʾ*), namely with the accompanying accidents. In that respect it is called a **mixed quiddity** (*al-māhiyya al-maḫlūṭa*), because it has been taken together with accidents that accompany it in existence. The example begins with human existence among external individuals, in which a quiddity is not encountered bereft of all determinations.","source_image":"source_images/I064.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I064.upper.02","volume":1,"page":64,"physical_pdf_page":77,"layer":"upper","title":"external individuals and the relation of their accidents","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«فإن وجود الأشخاص في الخارج» · «بين كرم وعرض من أفراد الإنسان».","arabic_status":"selected_visual_anchors_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The existence of individuals externally always occurs with particular qualifications and accidents—one individual may possess generosity, another some other attribute. Such differences are genuinely associated with existing persons, but they do not all enter the definition by which each is human. The discussion is not asserting that every accident is essential merely because no particular exists without accidents.","source_image":"source_images/I064.png","reading_status":"interpretive_first_pass_still_clause_incomplete","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemmas and Sanandaji sharh","stage":74,"fully_translated":false,"independently_proofed":false,"notes":["Original Arabic must be consulted; the English condenses some longer marginal syntax."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I065.lower.01","volume":1,"page":65,"physical_pdf_page":78,"layer":"lower","title":"The question of actuality and mental occurrence, unassigned lower commentary","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«المركب منها الذي اشتهر أن الفرد»؛ «ليس من التشخص كذلك»؛ «الوجود والاختصاص»؛ «قولـه لتجردها عنها»؛ «تسمى الماهية بشرط لا أيضا».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The individual is often described as a composite of the quiddity and its individuation, but this composite must not simply be confused with an entity in which individuation subsists apart from what is individuated. What is claimed to be excluded under the abstracting consideration needs care: an act of abstraction neither abolishes the thing's quiddity nor proves the existence of a bare quiddity outside. The lower writer calls attention to the language of particular existence, qualification, and individuation. In discussing “because it is abstracted from them,” he insists that abstraction *from concomitants* is an intellectual operation, not the appearance of a new concrete component corresponding to the abstracted aspect. The alleged absence of accidents is precisely the consideration under which the quiddity receives the technical label “taken on condition of no thing.”","source_image":"source_images/I065.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I065.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":65,"physical_pdf_page":78,"layer":"ruled","title":"The kinds of accident, separate short note","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«قوله لأن الوجود الخ»؛ «قسموا العارض للشيء قسمين»؛ «عارض الوجود»؛ «عارض الماهية»؛ «الوجود الخارجي»؛ «الوجود الذهني»؛ «كالكلية والجزئية».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"On “Because existence ...”: scholars have divided what supervenes on a thing into accidents of existence and accidents of quiddity. They further distinguish accidents associated with external existence, such as sensible characteristics, from accidents associated with mental existence, such as universality and particularity. What is being excluded by abstraction must therefore be specified: exclusion of independently external accidents is not identical to exclusion of anything attaching to a nature in the act of thinking. The accompanying note cautions against using the term “unconditioned” as though it meant a condition of exclusion. [The printed note's final short reference to the three conditions requires a more literal second reading.]","source_image":"source_images/I065.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I065.upper.01","volume":1,"page":65,"physical_pdf_page":78,"layer":"upper","title":"The quiddity taken on condition of abstraction","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«تؤخذ بشرط لا شيء»؛ «أي الخلو عن العوارض واللواحق»؛ «وسميت المجردة»؛ «ولا توجد في الأذهان فضلا عن الأعيان»؛ «لأن الوجود في الذهن ... من العوارض».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"A quiddity may be taken “on condition of no thing,” that is, conditioned by being free of concomitants and accidents. It is therefore called the **abstracted** quiddity, because of the abstraction of all such things from it. So understood, “it does not exist in minds, still less in external things.” For existence in the mind is itself an accident of the apprehended thing. This holds whether the accidents being excluded are all accidents without qualification or only those taken to be external, since mental existence also supplies a determination to what is conceived. The explanation turns on distinguishing the nature in itself from the features attaching to the nature *as apprehended*. The phrase continues onto I:66.","source_image":"source_images/I065.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I066.lower.01","volume":1,"page":66,"physical_pdf_page":79,"layer":"lower","title":"Mental abstraction, external accidents, and the apparent inconsistency","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«قيد العوارض بالخارجية جوز وجود المجردة في الذهن»؛ «المعروض الخارجية بهذا المعنى»؛ «سواء ...»؛ «فالماهية المجردة لا توجد من حيث هي مجردة»؛ «قولـه وأما الحكم عليها باستحالة وجودها».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"Restricting the excluded accidents to *external* accidents appears to allow an abstracted nature to exist mentally, since the mentally present nature need not bear the external accompaniments of its external instances. But the classification is not so straightforward: the mental determination itself is an accident for one purpose, and no such external accident for another. The note compares the manner in which intellects count or consider objects with the kinds of qualification inherited from external things. If all mental determinations are excluded as well, then the supposedly abstracted quiddity cannot have mental existence *under that restrictive consideration*. Yet the intellect may still grasp the meaning to which that condition is appended; otherwise it could not form the judgement that such an abstraction is impossible. This last point is the bridge to the next leaf.","source_image":"source_images/I066.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I066.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":66,"physical_pdf_page":79,"layer":"ruled","title":"What the expression “an accident” excludes","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«قوله بأن تعتبر له عارضا»؛ «ليس المراد من هذا أن هذا العارض ليس عارضا له أصلا»؛ «وإنما هو محض اعتبار الذهن له»؛ «من يحصل في الذهن أن ذلك العارض عارض له».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"On “that you consider it as having an accident”: the author's intention is not that the supposed accident is in no sense an accident of the object. Rather, the question is whether the intellect merely *considers* it as added, or whether it belongs to the way the object occurs in the mind and so cannot be omitted by an arbitrary stipulation. Conceiving a nature and then noticing its mentally supervening attributes are distinguishable acts, but the mental existence underlying the latter is not thereby eliminated. [Several pronouns and the note's final phrase need a second literal proof.]","source_image":"source_images/I066.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I066.upper.01","volume":1,"page":66,"physical_pdf_page":79,"layer":"upper","title":"Does mental conception itself violate the abstraction?","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الذهن»؛ «فإن الشيء الذي تفرضه موجودا في الذهن»؛ «يعرض له بنفس الوجود في الذهن»؛ «من غير أن يعتبر الذهن عارضا له»؛ «وأما الحكم عليها باستحالة وجودها فلا الحكم على الشيء إلا بعد تصوره».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The mental attribute under discussion does not have to be separately *attached* by an act of consideration: something posited as existing in thought acquires that determination through its very existence in thought, even without the intellect subsequently predicating it as an accident. But if it is said that the abstracted quiddity cannot exist, how can one make a judgement concerning it at all? A judgement about a thing presupposes some conception of the thing. It is not sufficient to declare the quiddity absolutely inconceivable; one must distinguish conceiving the nature and conceiving the nature *with the stipulated absence of every concomitant*. The argument continues in the ruled and lower comments.","source_image":"source_images/I066.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I067.lower.01","volume":1,"page":67,"physical_pdf_page":80,"layer":"lower","title":"Why there need not be a contradiction in conceiving what is judged impossible","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«قوله من حيث ذاتها»؛ «فلا تناقض»؛ «الماهية المجردة»؛ «الوجود في الذهن»؛ «الاستحالة».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The gloss objects that the apparent resolution still risks a contradiction. If the intellect judges a quiddity to be impossible *as abstracted*, it must apprehend what the expression designates. If its mental apprehension makes it mixed, what exactly is the subject of the impossibility judgement? The reply depends on whether a judgement of possibility is made about the nature considered absolutely, or about a nature under a condition of excluding all concomitants. Merely knowing a meaning does not establish that the precise *condition* added to it is instantiated in the same act of knowing. The writer explains that the two considerations can be distinguished, but warns that a switch from one to the other must not occur without notice.","source_image":"source_images/I067.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I067.lower.02","volume":1,"page":67,"physical_pdf_page":80,"layer":"lower","title":"The act of predication and the object of predication","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«المحكوم عليه»؛ «جواز الوجود»؛ «التجرد في الذهن»؛ «الوجود في الخارج»؛ «إذا كانا متحدين من وجه ...».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The subject of a proposition about possibility can be an intellectually signified nature, not an abstracted entity already realised in the mind in every respect specified in the proposition. It follows that saying “the abstracted quiddity is impossible” need not assert that the quiddity possesses no intelligible meaning; rather, the predicative subject is distinguished from the restriction about which the judgement is made. The marginal discussion considers different senses of “abstracted” and “mixed,” and argues that the answer must fix which sense is intended before claims about conceptual identity, external being or impossibility can be tested. [The last printed lines join the next leaf and include a difficult example that is not securely resolved here.]","source_image":"source_images/I067.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I067.upper.01","volume":1,"page":67,"physical_pdf_page":80,"layer":"upper","title":"The judgement “impossible” is made under a different consideration","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«كانت من حيث ذاتها مجردة مقارنة للمخلوطة»؛ «ومن حيث وجودها في الذهن قسما من المخلوطة»؛ «فيصح الحكم عليها باستحالة الوجود من حيث ذاتها»؛ «وقد تؤخذ لا بشرط شيء من مقارنة العوارض والتجرد عنها»؛ «وهي أعم من المخلوطة».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"A quiddity may be abstracted when considered in itself and yet, insofar as it is present in the mind, belong to what is mixed with determining features. We can therefore judge its existence impossible **under the consideration of sheer abstraction**, without denying that it can be apprehended under another consideration. It may further be taken “without the stipulation of anything”—neither stipulating the accompanying accidents nor stipulating abstraction from them. The nature so considered is more general than what is taken with accompanying accidents, because the latter is only one of the possible instantiations admitted by the former. The comparison continues on I:68.","source_image":"source_images/I067.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I068.lower.01","volume":1,"page":68,"physical_pdf_page":81,"layer":"lower","title":"What is being denied by “not a part of it”?","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«قوله لصدقها عليهما»؛ «مفهومه غير ذلك المفهوم»؛ «قوله لا جزءا منها»؛ «الماهية المخلوطة»؛ «الفرد»؛ «المطلقة».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The gloss first examines how the unrestricted nature applies both to what is accompanied and to what is abstracted, noting that a sameness of external subject does not imply the same mental meaning. It then objects to the statement that the nature is “not a part” of the composite. If the individual is made up of a quiddity and individuating features, one may describe quiddity as a constituent of the individual; but whether it is an independent part with its own external existence is another question. The use of “mixed” sometimes refers to the whole individual and sometimes to the quiddity qualified by concomitants. These cannot be interchanged without explaining how predication relates the part and whole.","source_image":"source_images/I068.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I068.lower.02","volume":1,"page":68,"physical_pdf_page":81,"layer":"lower","title":"The difference between predication and conceptual inclusion","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الوجود في الخارج»؛ «مطلق إنسان»؛ «زيد»؛ «ماهية من العوارض»؛ «القول».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"It is not necessary that an unrestricted human nature exist alongside Zayd as a second external entity before “human” can truly be predicated of Zayd. Conversely, the mere possibility of forming an unrestricted human concept does not prove that this concept, considered with its mental universal qualification, itself exists externally. The question is instead whether a nature predicated of an individual is the very nature in that individual, or merely one of its constituents. The marginal writer explores the alternative senses of “individual,” “absolute,” and “mixed,” which opens the more elaborate set of objections on I:69–70. [The final clause crosses I:68/I:69 and requires a second precise transcription pass.]","source_image":"source_images/I068.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I068.upper.01","volume":1,"page":68,"physical_pdf_page":81,"layer":"upper","title":"Unrestricted nature is not an externally separate part","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«وهي أعم من المجردة لصدقها على ما ...»؛ «وصدق المطلق على المقيد»؛ «فتوجد الماهية لا بشرط في الخارج لكونها نفسها ... أي نفس المخلوطة في الخارج»؛ «لا جزءا منها»؛ «إذ ليس الموجود من الإنسان إلا زيد وعمرو»؛ «فلا يصدق عليه المطلق لامتناع صدق الجزء الخارجي ...».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The nature taken without a condition is more general than the abstracted nature, since it can apply both when there are accompanying features and when such features are abstracted. This is the sense in which an unrestricted concept can be predicated of a restricted instance. The quiddity taken without stipulating anything “exists externally,” because it is the very quiddity found with its individuating concomitants—**not merely a part of that external composite**. Only particular humans, such as Zayd and ʿAmr, occur outside the intellect, but it would be wrong to infer that unconditioned humanity is a separately existing part in them. Rather, the nature under this conceptual consideration is predicated of individuals. If it were an externally detached constituent, predication of it as the whole could not straightforwardly be made. The argument continues on I:69.","source_image":"source_images/I068.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I069.lower.01","volume":1,"page":69,"physical_pdf_page":82,"layer":"lower","title":"Long marginal discussion, unassigned author: unrestricted and restricted","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«وحينئذ يتم القول بأن المطلقة نفس المخلوطة في الخارج لا جزؤها»؛ «وإنما التغاير في العقل»؛ «قوله دون الخارج ... أي بحسب المصدوق»؛ «وإنما التغاير بين المطلق والمقيد».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"On this basis the assertion is completed that the unrestricted nature is the very mixed nature externally, rather than a part of it: that is because the unrestricted nature does not need a separate external determination to distinguish it from the mixed nature. The distinction lies in thought. The words “not externally” may be read with reference to what the two concepts apply to, rather than as a denial that their *meanings* differ. The commentator had explained “mixed” in one sense, while an objector took it otherwise; the objection therefore risks shifting between the mixed quiddity as an entire compound and the unrestricted nature as considered without its qualification. As for the unrestricted human being and one restricted by some particular specification, one cannot simply infer that the unrestricted human subsists as an external item distinct from the restricted human. [The precise syntax of the initial reference to the author and commentator requires a clearer reading.]","source_image":"source_images/I069.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I069.lower.02","volume":1,"page":69,"physical_pdf_page":82,"layer":"lower","title":"The mixed nature as whole, or the nature as a part","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«لو كانت المخلوطة جزءا للفرد في الخارج»؛ «المطلقة ... لم يصح ما يأتي»؛ «المخلوطة هي الأفراد في الخارج»؛ «لا يصح حمل نحو الإنسان على زيد مثلا»؛ «بأن يقال الفرد قد يطلق ويراد به المجموع».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"If one were to say that the mixed quiddity is only a *part* of an individual existing externally, the unrestricted quiddity, insofar as it coincides with that mixed quiddity, would likewise become a mere part; the subsequent predication would then fail. Moreover, the claim that the mixed quiddity just *is* the external individuals must be carefully distinguished from saying that all the parts of a complex individual are each identical to the entire individual. Otherwise it would be impossible to predicate “human” of Zayd, when such predication is plainly accepted. One answer is that the word “individual” may denote the entire composite, while in another use it may denote the quiddity together with the specifying accidents. The author warns that distinctions in conceptual consideration must not be turned into unsupported distinctions between independently existing external objects.","source_image":"source_images/I069.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I069.lower.03","volume":1,"page":69,"physical_pdf_page":82,"layer":"lower","title":"Reply to the remark about the nature “taken without a condition”","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«قوله ثم إذا اعتبرت المأخوذة لا بشرط»؛ «إن المأخوذ لا بشرط يمتنع أن يوجد في الخارج لأنه كلي طبيعي»؛ «لأن الوجود فيه يستلزم التشخص المنافي للكلية»؛ «وتنافي اللوازم دليل ...».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"On “Then if the thing taken without a condition is considered ...”: it can be objected that what is taken without a condition cannot exist externally if its being a natural universal is understood as requiring universality itself, since every external existent must be individuated, and individuation conflicts with the mental universal taken as universal. The implication of the conflicting consequences is itself evidence that the respective premises cannot all be maintained together. This objection is continued, with its answer, at the start of I:70.\n\n## I:70 — The quiddity exists externally, but the mental accident of universality does not\n*Physical combined PDF page 83.*","source_image":"source_images/I069.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I069.upper.01","volume":1,"page":69,"physical_pdf_page":82,"layer":"upper","title":"Upper, Taftāzānī lemma embedded in Sanandajī's commentary","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الوجود الكلي عليه»؛ «وإنما ذلك التغاير بين المطلق والمقيد في العقل دون الخارج»؛ «ثم إذا اعتبرت المأخوذة لا بشرط شيء معروضة للكلي المنطقي»؛ «فهو ... الكلي الطبيعي»؛ «وإنما يوجد منه المعروض دون الكلي العارض»؛ «مجردا عن العوارض ... من الكلية والجنسية والنوعية».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"—the universal's existence [or its predication] of it. “That,” namely the distinction between what is unrestricted and what is restricted, obtains **in the intellect**, not externally. “Then if” the quiddity “taken without stipulating anything” is considered as something to which the logical universal is predicated, it is—the quiddity under that consideration—the **natural universal**. What exists externally of it is “that which receives the predicate,” that is, the quiddity of which universality is predicated, “not the universal that is predicated of it.” This last item is the abstract attribute of universality, not an additional external thing. One might object that the quiddity too should have no external existence, since the predicated universality excludes it. But the quiddity is not present externally *stripped of all accidents*: universality, genus-character and species-character, and similar attributes are mental accidents which, considered as such, preclude external existence. This clarifies the celebrated saying that the natural universal has existence outside: its externally present subject is not the external instantiation of the *mental predicate* of universality. The sentence continues on I:70.","source_image":"source_images/I069.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I070.lower.01","volume":1,"page":70,"physical_pdf_page":83,"layer":"lower","title":"The objection and answer to I:69, unassigned margin","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«تنافي الملزومات»؛ «مجرد المأخوذ لا بشرط ليس كليا طبيعيا بل مع اعتبار كونه معروضا للكلي المنطقي»؛ «والوجود هو مجرد ذلك من غير اعتبار الكلية»؛ «وأما المأخوذ مع عارض الكلية فلا وجود له في الخارج».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"—the incompatibility of the necessary consequences establishes incompatibility in the things that entail them. The answer, in summary, is that the thing *merely* taken without a condition is not thereby a natural universal; rather, it becomes one when considered as the subject of the logical universal. External existence belongs to the subject as such, without including the mental universality among its constitutive features, and that existence is realised when individuating accidents arise. What is taken together with universality as an accident has no external existence. This is the intended sense of the familiar statement that the natural universal exists externally. Consider it carefully: the explanation would not permit an unrestricted quiddity to float free of its external instantiations.","source_image":"source_images/I070.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I070.lower.02","volume":1,"page":70,"physical_pdf_page":83,"layer":"lower","title":"Mixed quiddity is not unambiguously an external composite","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«قوله فالوجود حقيقة هي المخلوطة وهي الأفراد»؛ «لا يجوز أن تكون عبارة عن المقيدة المقترنة بالعوارض لا المجموع»؛ «كان الأول معتبرا مطلقا والآخر مقيدا»؛ «أجزاء الكل موجودة بوجود واحد»؛ «فلا محيص إلا بأن يقال أن المخلوطة هي المجموع المذكور كالفرد».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"On “What truly exists is the mixed quiddity—that is, the individuals”: this is correct if the mixed quiddity is taken to mean the composite whole. It must not simply be made identical with the nature qualified by accidents while excluding the whole, since such usage allows the nature, in thought, to be a constituent of a compound without therefore being a separate constituent existing in reality. Were one part unrestricted and another restricted by something else, one could speak of them as different conceptual parts; yet externally the parts of a whole need not possess a separately multiplied existence. One may instead say that the quiddity and its accompanying accidents subsist together with one external existence; or that several existences are attributed to constituents. The claim “mixed” thus requires an explicit account of what sort of composition is meant, and whether “individual” stands for the whole or for the qualified nature. The author's language is examined on both possible readings, rather than assuming a conclusion.","source_image":"source_images/I070.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I070.lower.03","volume":1,"page":70,"physical_pdf_page":83,"layer":"lower","title":"Transition to Ibn Sīnā, unassigned margin","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«قوله والقائل ابن سينا»؛ «الماهية قد تؤخذ بشرط لا شيء بأن يتصور معناها بشرط أن يكون ذلك المعنى وحده».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"On “The one who says it is Ibn Sīnā”: the quiddity may be taken on condition that no thing be with it—meaning that its signification is conceived on its own, with the stipulation that that meaning be considered alone. The note's detailed account proceeds across the page boundary.\n\n## I:71 — Matter, subject, genus and the meaning of conditional quiddity\n*Physical combined PDF page 84.*","source_image":"source_images/I070.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I070.upper.01","volume":1,"page":70,"physical_pdf_page":83,"layer":"upper","title":"Upper commentary: existence of the externally individuated nature","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«أما صدقه هو عليه وهو المأخوذ لا بشرط شيء فموجود فيه»؛ «ووجوده فيه إنما هو عند عروض التشخص»؛ «فالوجود حقيقة هي المخلوطة»؛ «وهي الأفراد»؛ «وأما المأخوذ مع عارض الكلية فلا توجد»؛ «الكلي العقلي».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"As for its being truly predicated of that subject, the thing taken without stipulating anything does exist in it. Its existence there, however, only obtains when individuation supervenes. Accordingly, the actually existent quiddity is the **mixed** quiddity, namely the individuals. The feminine pronoun may be explained through the grammatical predicate, or because its referent is the quiddity. But a quiddity considered along with the accident of universality does not exist externally; nor does the compound of quiddity and that mental accident, namely the **intellectual universal** (*al-kullī al-ʿaqlī*).","source_image":"source_images/I070.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I070.upper.02","volume":1,"page":70,"physical_pdf_page":83,"layer":"upper","title":"Upper commentary: Ibn Sīnā's alternative terminology","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«ثم ما ذكر من معنى الماهية بشرط لا شيء هو المشهور»؛ «وقد يقال»؛ «والقائل ابن سينا»؛ «الماهية بشرط ...».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"What has just been given as the meaning of a quiddity “taken on condition of no thing” is the familiar account. “But it has also been said”—the proponent here is Ibn Sīnā—that the quiddity is taken “on condition that ...”. The expression is completed, and its new meaning worked out, on I:71. The alternative must not be silently equated with the common later terminology.","source_image":"source_images/I070.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I071.lower.01","volume":1,"page":71,"physical_pdf_page":84,"layer":"lower","title":"Does the material part precede the composite in both existences?","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«ولا يكون المعنى الأول مقولا على المجموع مع حال المقارنة بل جزء منه مادة له»؛ «قوله مثلا الحيوان إذا أخذناه بشرط أن يكون معه الخ»؛ «هذا هو المذكور بقوله وقد يقال الماهية الخ».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"The first meaning is not predicated of the composite while the accompanying feature is present. It is instead its part and serves as matter for it. The passage concerning animal taken with a further condition is an exposition of the alternative account introduced by “It has also been said concerning the quiddity ...”. Yet this depends on taking the example exactly as specified, not on treating every use of “animal” as denoting the same concept in the same respect.","source_image":"source_images/I071.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I071.lower.02","volume":1,"page":71,"physical_pdf_page":84,"layer":"lower","title":"Objection: a composite is not necessarily an exterior part plus an interior part","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«ومعناه على ما سبق آنفا»؛ «حيث لو اقترن به النطق كان النطق زائدا عليه وصار المجموع مركبا من الحيوان والناطق»؛ «فلا تكون الحيوان مقولا على ذلك المجموع بل كان جزءا ومادة له»؛ «ما هو زائد عليه متناقضا».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"If rationality is associated with animality, then on the restricted reading rationality is an addition to it and the whole is a composite of animal and rational. Animality so construed cannot be predicated of the whole; it is a constituent and its matter. But if “animal” is instead treated simply as that which can have something accompanying it, whether the concomitant is an accident or something else, the conclusion cannot be transferred without argument. At issue is whether the addition is part of the thing's definition, or only a condition under which the thing is apprehended; confusing the two makes the assertion “animal is the whole” contradict “animal is its part.”","source_image":"source_images/I071.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I071.lower.03","volume":1,"page":71,"physical_pdf_page":84,"layer":"lower","title":"Objection concerning genus, species and differentia","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«قوله كان نوعا»؛ «واعترض عليه بأن النوع مجموع الجنس والفصل»؛ «فكيف جعل عبارة عن المأخوذ بشرط شيء»؛ «وأجيب بأنه تسامح مبني على أن الجنس والنوع والفصل واحد بالذات لكن ...».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"On “it is a species”: an objection is raised that the species is the combination of genus and differentia. How, then, can species be identified with the genus taken under the stipulation that the differentia accompany it? One reply describes this as a permissible looseness of expression, founded on the claim that genus, species and differentia are one in their external subject, while intellectually distinguished. The marginal writer stresses, however, that their unity in subject does not make their *conceptual definitions* interchangeable. The discussion passes into the next page.\n\n## I:72 — The closure of the threefold example and an extended marginal critique\n*Physical combined PDF page 85. Only a short upper line is printed; almost the entire remaining page belongs to the long marginal text.*","source_image":"source_images/I071.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I071.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":71,"physical_pdf_page":84,"layer":"ruled","title":"Separately printed short note (not Sanandajī's sharḥ)","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«قوله في الوجودين الخ»؛ «واعلم أنهم اختلفوا أن الكل موجود في الخارج أولا بل الموجود فيه هي الأفراد فقط»؛ «وكلام المصنف هنا ظاهر في الثاني».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"On “in the two modes of existence, etc.”: know that they have disagreed as to whether the universal exists in the external world in the first place, or whether only individual instances exist there. The author's wording here appears to favour the latter. This note should be kept distinct from both the surrounding main text and the lengthy lower annotation.","source_image":"source_images/I071.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I071.upper.01","volume":1,"page":71,"physical_pdf_page":84,"layer":"upper","title":"Upper commentary: a nature taken with a stipulation of separateness","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«لا شيء بمعنى أن يفرض عليها كل ما يقارنها»؛ «فتكون مادة للشخص»؛ «متقدمة عليه في الوجودين»؛ «فالمراد من التقدم هو التقدم بالطبع»؛ «ولا يصح حملها على الشخص لانتفاء شرط الحمل وهو الاتحاد في الوجود».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"—“no thing,” in the sense that whatever accompanies the quiddity is assumed to be additional to it, and the quiddity is considered apart from those accompanying features. “It then becomes matter” and a constituent “of the individual,” understood as the composite of the nature and what accompanies it, “prior to it in both kinds of existence,” external and mental, since a whole cannot be realised without its constituent. The priority intended is **priority by nature**, not temporal priority. Nor may it be predicated of the individual under that consideration, because the condition of predication—unity in existence—does not obtain.","source_image":"source_images/I071.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I071.upper.02","volume":1,"page":71,"physical_pdf_page":84,"layer":"upper","title":"Upper commentary: the example of animal and rational","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الحيوان إذا أخذناه بشرط أن لا يكون معه الناطق»؛ «كان جزءا ومادة للمجموع المركب من الحيوان والناطق ولا يحمل عليه»؛ «وإذا أخذناه بشرط أن يكون معه الناطق كان نوعا»؛ «وإذا أخذناه لا بشرط أن يكون معه شيء كان له جهتان».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"Thus, when we take *animal* on condition that the rational does not accompany it, it is a part and a material constituent of the composite of animal and rational, and is not predicated of that composite. When we take animal on condition that rationality accompanies it, it becomes a **species**. When we take it without stipulating whether anything accompanies it, it admits two aspects: we can consider the difference between it and what accompanies it, or consider their unity. The first animal is thus a part of the human being, preceding it in both modes of existence, and cannot be predicated of it. The contrast between the second and third cases closes in I:72.","source_image":"source_images/I071.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I072.lower.01","volume":1,"page":72,"physical_pdf_page":85,"layer":"lower","title":"The proposed resolution and the commentator's disagreement","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«فيه تأمل والتحقيق ما ذكر»؛ «قوله والثالث جنس له محمول الخ»؛ «حقيقة الكلام أن المأخوذ لا بشرط شيء إذا اعتبر بحسب أنحاء ...»؛ «هذا يخالف ما في الشرح نوع مخالفة فتأمله».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"There is room for reflection here, and the correct resolution requires further precision. On “The third is a genus predicated of it”: it has been said that the real account is as follows. If the nature taken without stipulating anything is considered with respect both to its difference from an accompaniment and to its unity with it, it can be an essential constituent capable of predication. Considered under sheer unity, it may be taken as a species. I say, however, that this differs in a certain respect from the account given in the commentary. Consider that difference carefully. The comparison must therefore not be presented as an unqualified agreement between Ibn Sīnā and the later terminology.","source_image":"source_images/I072.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I072.lower.02","volume":1,"page":72,"physical_pdf_page":85,"layer":"lower","title":"What precisely is meant by 'without stipulating'?","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«لا يشترط أن يكون ذلك المعنى وحده ولا وحده مع تجويز أن يقارنه غيره وأن لا يقارنه»؛ «ويكون المعنى الأول مقولا على المجموع حال المقارنة»؛ «قد يكون غير متحصل بنفسه»؛ «بل يكون مبهما محتملا للمقولية على أشياء مختلفة الحقائق».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"A meaning taken without a condition does **not** stipulate that it be alone, nor that it be alone with the additional requirement that some other thing may or may not accompany it. Rather, both accompaniment and non-accompaniment are left open. Under this consideration the first meaning may be predicated of a composite when an accompaniment is present; it need not be independently complete by itself. It can remain indeterminate and open to predication of things with different essential realities. Its further completion arises from what is conjoined to it, enabling it to be specified and become, in some instances, one of several genuinely distinct natures.","source_image":"source_images/I072.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I072.lower.03","volume":1,"page":72,"physical_pdf_page":85,"layer":"lower","title":"Animal as genus, and the source of the problem","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«كما في الأنواع الداخلة تحت الجنس وهو نوع مثلا إذا أخذ الحيوان بشرط أن لا يكون معه شيء»؛ «وإن اقترن به ناطق صار المجموع مركبا من الحيوان والناطق»؛ «وإذا أخذ بشرط أن لا يكون معه الناطق مختصا ومتحصلا به كان نوعا»؛ «وإذا أخذ لا بشرط أن يكون معه شيء».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"This may be illustrated by the species falling under a genus. If we consider “animal” under the condition of not including any further thing, then, when rationality accompanies it, a composite of animal and rational results; animal in that respect is its material component rather than something predicated of the completed whole. If it is taken together with rationality as specifying and completing it, it is the species. If it is taken without stipulating what may accompany it, the relation to completion remains open. The controversy is about whether “animal” in each of these examples stands for the same conceived nature or for a nature taken under an additional condition. The latter would make apparently simple claims of identity or priority equivocal.","source_image":"source_images/I072.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I072.lower.04","volume":1,"page":72,"physical_pdf_page":85,"layer":"lower","title":"Matter, priority, and conceptual versus external order","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«الأول جزء الإنسان مقدم عليه في الوجودين والثاني نفس الإنسان والثالث جنس محمول عليه»؛ «لا يكون جزءا لأن الجزء لا يحمل على الكل بالمواطأة»؛ «الفصل أنه جزء من النوع»؛ «أنه لا بد للعقل من ملاحظتهما في تحصيل صورة مطابقة للنوع»؛ «فيكون متقدما على النوع في العقل»؛ «وأما بحسب الخارج فيكون متأخرا لأنه ما لم يوجد الإنسان في الخارج لم يعقل له شيء يعمه وغيره».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"Thus the first [animal] is a constituent of the human being and prior to it in the two modes of existence, the second is the human being itself, and the third is a genus predicated of it. Nevertheless, a constituent, precisely insofar as it is a constituent, cannot be predicated of the whole *univocally* as though it were identical with the whole. The genus and differentia must be distinguished in their role in an intellectual definition: each must be considered in order to form a concept adequate to the species, and in that respect the constituent is prior to the species in understanding. In the external order, however, the nature as an abstract genus is not found prior to concrete humans; the argument therefore cannot simply infer an independent external genus from its conceptual priority. The last lines connect to I:73, which is a full leaf of continuing marginal discussion, not a new principal-text page.","source_image":"source_images/I072.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"unassigned printed marginal or ruled annotation","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I072.upper.01","volume":1,"page":72,"physical_pdf_page":85,"layer":"upper","title":"The whole upper text on the leaf","arabic":null,"arabic_anchors":"«عليه والثاني نفس الإنسان والثالث جنس محمول عليه (ثم) الماهية البسيطة».","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","english":"—of it. The second [consideration] is the very human being, and the third is a genus predicated of it. “Then” [the discussion passes to] the simple quiddity. The running principal text then jumps to I:74, because I:73 contains only marginal commentary.","source_image":"source_images/I072.png","reading_status":"source_facing_connected_first_pass_not_clause_exhaustive","attribution":"mixed Taftazani lemma and Sanandaji sharh","stage":73,"notes":["DO NOT CLAIM full all-register literal translation, second-pass certification or independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I073.lower.01","volume":1,"page":73,"physical_pdf_page":86,"layer":"lower","title":"Three different senses of quiddity “taken with a condition”","english":"—Abū ʿAlī [Ibn Sīnā] [discussed it] in the Shifāʾ, and the investigator summarized it in his commentary on the Ishārāt. End [of the citation]. The author, may God have mercy on him, then mentioned that it presents points for discussion; we have already indicated some of them earlier, so refer back to that passage. Once this is understood, know that the distinctions given at the beginning of the chapter are the customary ways in which later scholars consider quiddity. Yet the Chief Master's reported words may suggest ways of considering it other than those that have become customary. In his usage, quiddity taken on condition of nothing is the meaning conceived in isolation: were something to accompany it, that accompanying thing would be reckoned external to the meaning and not included in it. Accordingly, when they are conjoined the meaning is not predicated of the resulting composite as a whole, but is a constituent part of it—its matter.","arabic":"أبو علي في الشفاء ولخصه المحقق في شرح الإشارات انتهى ثم ذكر المصنف رحمه الله أن فيه مواضع بحث وإنا قد أشرنا إلى بعضها فيما سبق فلينظر إذا تقرر هذا فاعلم أن ما سبق أوائل الفصل هي الاعتبارات المشهورة للماهية بين المتأخرين وأما الشيخ الرئيس فقد يستفاد من كلامه المنقول اعتبارات غير ما اشتهر وهي أن الماهية المأخوذة بشرط لا شيء هي ما يتصور معناها وحده بحيث لو قارنها شيء اعتبر ذلك الذي قارنها خارجا عنه غير داخل فيه فلا يكون ذلك المعنى مقولا على المجموع المركب حال المقارنة بل جزءا منه ومادته","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"full_visible_page_attempted_with_unresolved_readings_not_final","source_image":"source_images/I073.png","source_image_sha256":"45d1f82cac3e5f6c5ddac1344db18f39b00956d647d1c2c52ce61011d341dac0","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":["incoming I072.lower.final. The whole of printed I:73 is marginal continuation; there is **no newly printed principal upper text** on this page."],"source_kind":"continuous_all_visible_Arabic_draft_first_pass_with_marked_uncertainties","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"prior_english_stage77":"—[This account is attributed to] Abū ʿAlī [Ibn Sīnā] in the *Shifāʾ*, and [the investigator] summed it up in his commentary on the *Ishārāt*. End of citation. The author, may God have mercy on him, then said that there are points in it admitting discussion. We have already indicated some of them; consult what precedes. With that established, know that the considerations stated at the beginning of the chapter are the **familiar classifications of quiddity among later scholars**. But one may infer from the Chief Master's quoted wording considerations other than those conventionally reported. In one sense, *quiddity taken on condition of nothing* (*bi-sharṭ lā shayʾ*) is the meaning conceived **by itself alone**. If something accompanies it, the accompanying thing is regarded as external to it, not included within it. The meaning under this consideration cannot be predicated of the composite whole once another thing has been conjoined to it; rather, it will be a **part** or **material element** of that whole.","prior_arabic_anchors_stage77":"«أبو علي في الشفاء وخلصه المحقق في شرح الإشارات انتهى» · «ثم ذكر المصنف رحمه الله أن فيه مواضع بحث» · «ما سبق أوائل الفصل هي الاعتبارات المشهورة للماهية بين المتأخرين» · «وأما الشيخ الرئيس فقد يستفاد من كلامه المنقول اعتبارات غير ما اشتهر» · «الماهية المأخوذة بشرط لا شيء هي ما يتصور معناها وحده».","source_visual_check":"original Vol I p.73 (combined physical PDF p.86); same-editor image comparison; uncertain words remain","uncertain_readings":["وإنا قد أشرنا/وإذا قد أشرنا: check short connective on printed line 2","اعتبر ذلك الذي خارجا عنه: syntax and exact particle need reproof"],"critical_apparatus":[{"kind":"transcription_correction","prior_reading":"اعتبر ذلك الذي خارجا عنه غير داخل فيه","current_reading":"اعتبر ذلك الذي قارنها خارجا عنه غير داخل فيه","basis":"Būlāq I:73, printed line 5 (native scan). Earlier transcript dropped قارنها, yielding defective syntax. Compare quoted Ibn Sina passage in Sharḥ al-Ishārāt; not an independent Taqrīb witness.","confidence":"direct_source_image","independent_witness":false}]},{"id":"I073.lower.02","volume":1,"page":73,"physical_pdf_page":86,"layer":"lower","title":"“Taken with something” and “taken without a condition”","english":"Quiddity taken on condition of something, as indicated by its contrast with the preceding case, is the meaning conceived not in isolation. If something accompanies it, that thing is considered included in it; the meaning thus becomes identical with the composite whole in the circumstance of their conjunction. Quiddity taken without a condition, by contrast, is likewise not considered in isolation, but if something accompanies it neither its inclusion nor its exclusion is stipulated. The meaning consequently remains indeterminate and capable of being predicated of the whole composite when the conjunction obtains. [The Arabic term for predicability is proposed from a parallel, not independently collated.]","arabic":"والماهية المأخوذة بشرط شيء بقرينة تقابلها بما سبق ما يتصور معناها لا وحده بحيث لو قارنها شيء اعتبر داخلا فيها فيكون ذلك المعنى عين المجموع المركب حال المقارنة والمأخوذة لا بشرط شيء ما يتصور معناها لا وحده بحيث لو قارنها شيء لم يشترط دخوله فيها ولا عدم دخوله فيكون مبهما محتملا ⟦للمقولية⟧ على المجموع حال المقارنة","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"full_visible_page_attempted_with_unresolved_readings_not_final","source_image":"source_images/I073.png","source_image_sha256":"45d1f82cac3e5f6c5ddac1344db18f39b00956d647d1c2c52ce61011d341dac0","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":["The long marginal argument includes subtle changes between *predicated of the whole* and *a component of the whole*. English has been kept tied to those conceptual alternatives. Certain compressed words between the three definitions need a second line-level comparison."],"source_kind":"continuous_all_visible_Arabic_draft_first_pass_with_marked_uncertainties","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"prior_english_stage77":"Quiddity *taken on condition of something* (*bi-sharṭ shayʾ*), understood in contrast to the preceding case, is conceived so that the accompanying qualification is **counted within it**; the original meaning is then treated as the composite whole in the circumstance of conjunction. By contrast, quiddity *taken without condition* (*lā bi-sharṭ*) is not conceived with the stipulation that the accompanying thing be included, nor with the stipulation that it be excluded. Its meaning remains **unspecified as regards inclusion**, capable of being predicated of the whole in which another qualification is present, and not defined as an intrinsically separate part of that whole. The distinction is a distinction in how the meaning is considered, not proof of additional externally existing constituents.","prior_arabic_anchors_stage77":"«والماهية المأخوذة بشرط شيء بقرينة تقابلها بما سبق ما يتصور معناها لا وحده بحيث لو قارنها شيء اعتبر داخلا فيها» · «فيكون ذلك المعنى عين المجموع المركب حال المقارنة» · «والمأخوذة لا بشرط شيء ما يتصور معناها لا وحده» · «ولا عدم دخوله فيكون مبهما محتملا للقسمة على المجموع حال المقارنة».","source_visual_check":"original Vol I p.73 (combined physical PDF p.86); same-editor image comparison; uncertain words remain","uncertain_readings":["The word after محتملا resembles للمقولية but has not been independently confirmed; marked ⟦...؟⟧ rather than passed off as a certified reading","The verb لا يشترط/لم يشترط needs precise consonantal check"],"critical_apparatus":[{"kind":"transcription_correction","prior_reading":"لو قارنها شيء لا يشترط دخوله","current_reading":"لو قارنها شيء لم يشترط دخوله","basis":"Būlāq I:73, printed line 9. Native image reads لم يشترط rather than لا يشترط; semantic force is unchanged but the written text is now matched.","confidence":"direct_source_image","independent_witness":false},{"kind":"parallel_supported_proposed_reading","printed_reading":"ink compressed at this locus; likely للمقولية","editorial_reading":"للمقولية","basis":"Būlāq I:73 local image; exact quoted formulation in Taftāzānī, Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, vol. I p.100: مبهما محتملا للمقولية; related Ibn Sīnā formulation. Parallel only, not independent Taqrīb witness.","independent_witness":false,"confidence":"probable_not_final"}]},{"id":"I073.lower.03","volume":1,"page":73,"physical_pdf_page":86,"layer":"lower","title":"Animal, human, and the genus/species illustration","english":"Thus, when animal [animality] is considered in the first sense, it is a part of the composite—for example, the human being. Considered in the second sense, it constitutes a species, the human being, for example. Considered in the third, it becomes a genus predicable of the human being and of other, different realities that share animality. On this terminology, quiddity taken on condition of something—the one called the mixed [or conditioned] quiddity—is the composite of the quiddity and what accompanies it. It is therefore identical with the individuals, without the earlier objection necessarily following.","arabic":"فالحيوان إذا اعتبر بالمعنى الأول يكون جزءا للمركب الذي هو الإنسان مثلا وإذا اعتبر بالمعنى الثاني يكون نوعا هو الإنسان مثلا وإذا اعتبر بالمعنى الثالث يكون جنسا مقولا على الإنسان وغيره من الحقائق المختلفة المشتركة في الحيوانية فعلى هذا الاصطلاح تكون الماهية المأخوذة بشرط شيء المسماة بالمخلوطة عبارة عن المركب من الماهية وما يقارنها فتكون عين الأفراد من غير لزوم ما سبق من الاعتراض","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"full_visible_page_attempted_with_unresolved_readings_not_final","source_image":"source_images/I073.png","source_image_sha256":"45d1f82cac3e5f6c5ddac1344db18f39b00956d647d1c2c52ce61011d341dac0","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":[],"source_kind":"continuous_all_visible_Arabic_draft_first_pass_with_marked_uncertainties","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"prior_english_stage77":"For example, if “animal” is considered in the **first** sense, it is a part of the composite that is a human being. Considered in the **second** sense, it may be the species human [under the composite consideration]. Considered in the **third** sense, it is a **genus predicable of the human being and of others**, the different realities that share animality. On this usage, the quiddity taken under a restrictive condition may be a component of the composite, while what is taken without that condition can be a genus shared among individuals. Yet the conclusion must not be generalized to every logical combination without checking which meaning of “part,” “species” and “genus” is actually under discussion.","prior_arabic_anchors_stage77":"«إذا اعتبر المعنى الأول يكون جزءا للمركب الذي هو الإنسان مثلا» · «وإذا اعتبر المعنى الثاني يكون نوعا هو الإنسان مثلا» · «وإذا اعتبر بالمعنى الثالث يكون جنسا مقولا على الإنسان وغيره» · «الحقائق المختلفة المشتركة في الحيوانية».","source_visual_check":"original Vol I p.73 (combined physical PDF p.86); same-editor image comparison; uncertain words remain","uncertain_readings":["The phrase المسماة بالمخلوطة is printed with compressed dots; its precise vocalization and editorial punctuation require confirmation"]},{"id":"I073.lower.04","volume":1,"page":73,"physical_pdf_page":86,"layer":"lower","title":"Matter, form, and why the alleged genus is not automatically a real part","english":"Nor is it then necessary to appeal to the author's earlier statement that calling the species “what is taken on condition of something” is merely a latitude of expression, based on species, genus and differentia being one in the underlying thing. Even if that holds for quiddity in relation to the species beneath it, its relation to varieties and individuals still needs consideration. Reflect. Quiddity taken without condition—the unrestricted quiddity—remains indeterminate and open to predication. In that respect it is a genus or a common accident; it is not, considered simply under that concept, identical with what it is truly said of or a part of that thing. Nevertheless, in respect of actual denotation, it cannot but be either a constituent or identical with its instances. As noted above, the concepts exclude one another under the distinct descriptions, while their ranges of predication stand in relations of greater and lesser generality.","arabic":"ومن غير احتياج إلى ما سبق من المصنف من أن جعل النوع عبارة عن المأخوذة بشرط شيء مسامحة مبنية على كون النوع والجنس والفصل واحدا بالذات فإنه إذا تم في الماهية بالنسبة إلى الأنواع دونها بالنسبة إلى الأصناف والأشخاص فتأمل وتكون الماهية لا بشرط شيء أعني المطلقة مبهمة محتملة ⟦للمقولية؟⟧ ومن هذه الحيثية تكون جنسا أو عرضا عاما لا عينا لما صدقها ولا جزءا منها وإن كانت من حيث الصدق لا تخلو من أن تكون جزءا أو عينا لها كما سبق أنها من حيث المفهوم متنافية ومن حيث الصدق أعم منها","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"full_visible_page_attempted_with_unresolved_readings_not_final","source_image":"source_images/I073.png","source_image_sha256":"45d1f82cac3e5f6c5ddac1344db18f39b00956d647d1c2c52ce61011d341dac0","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":["The compressed phrase naming the “mixed” or “conditioned” quiddity requires visual proof before its Arabic wording is fixed; the above is a working conceptual rendering, not a silently reconstructed quotation."],"source_kind":"continuous_all_visible_Arabic_draft_first_pass_with_marked_uncertainties","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"prior_english_stage77":"If quiddity is taken with or apart from conditions in the different senses just described, the result can be described as the composite of a meaning with its accompanying qualifications, or as the individual things of which that meaning is said. The familiar objection—that the compound would then need a second constituent in addition to its asserted quiddity—does not follow automatically. Nor is there always a need to say, as the author previously did, that calling the species “the quiddity taken on condition of something” is merely a concession of usage. The logical status of the same meaning changes with the **level of comparison**: it may function as genus relative to what falls below it and as species relative to what falls above it, and when it is considered in relation to kinds, varieties and individual persons, the relations change accordingly. The commentator is distinguishing the conceptual function of these classifications rather than supposing that each name establishes an independent part outside the mind.","prior_arabic_anchors_stage77":"«تكون الماهية المأخوذة بشرط لا شيء المسماة بالمخلوطة عبارة عن المركب من الماهية وما يقارنها» · «فتكون عين الأفراد من غير لزوم ما سبق من الاعتراض» · «ولا احتياج إلى ما سبق من المصنف من أن جعل النوع عبارة عن المأخوذ بشرط شيء مسامحة» · «لأن الماهية بالنسبة إلى الأنواع دونها بالنسبة إلى الأصناف والأشخاص».","source_visual_check":"original Vol I p.73 (combined physical PDF p.86); same-editor image comparison; uncertain words remain","uncertain_readings":["The predicate after محتملة in the source is unclear (القابلية/للقولية); original image must be checked again before normalization","The phrase فإنه إذا تم في الماهية بالنسبة إلى الأنواع is syntactically compressed; English clause remains provisional","لا عينها لما صدقها / لا عينا لما صدقها requires grammatical proof"],"critical_apparatus":[{"kind":"unresolved_term_with_strong_parallel","printed_reading":"⟦للمقولية؟⟧","proposed_reading":"للمقولية","basis":"Comparable technical usage in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid I:100; Būlāq I:73 local glyphs remain congested. Do not silently emend."}]},{"id":"I073.lower.05","volume":1,"page":73,"physical_pdf_page":86,"layer":"lower","title":"Universal predication, qualification, and the wording of the *Maqāṣid*","english":"It follows that treating the non-indeterminate [restricted] cases as divisions of this quiddity is legitimate only when one considers what the expressions apply to. Hence the author's statement in the Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, referring to what has preceded, that the first is more general than the second means that it is more general with respect to denotation, not with respect to conceptual meaning. A quiddity considered without stipulating that something accompany it is a predicable genus only insofar as it is considered indeterminately. What matters is not a stipulation that the accompanying feature be inside it or outside it, but the possibility of considering it in the appropriate way. [The concluding phrase is elliptical and may continue conceptually on the next leaf.]","arabic":"فظهر أن جعل غير المبهم من أقسام هذه الماهية إنما هو من حيث الصدق فمعنى قول المصنف في شرح المقاصد أن معنى ذلك على ما مر من كون الأول أعم من الثاني هو أنه كذلك بحسب الصدق لا المفهوم فالمأخوذة لا بشرط أن يكون معه شيء إنما يكون جنسا محمولا إذا كان معتبرا على سبيل الإبهام بحيث يكون فيها لا أن يعتبر المقارن له داخلا فيه وخارجا عنه فالمعتبر فيه هو صلاحية اعتباره","arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"full_visible_page_attempted_with_unresolved_readings_not_final","source_image":"source_images/I073.png","source_image_sha256":"45d1f82cac3e5f6c5ddac1344db18f39b00956d647d1c2c52ce61011d341dac0","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":["outgoing I074.lower.01."],"source_kind":"continuous_all_visible_Arabic_draft_first_pass_with_marked_uncertainties","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"prior_english_stage77":"Considered **as meanings**, some of these classifications stand in opposition; considered **in respect of predication**, one can be more general than another. The lower commentator accordingly questions the division that places an unrestricted meaning and its qualified instances within a single univocal taxonomy. A phrase in *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid* that calls the first sense broader than the second should not be interpreted without specifying whether “broader” concerns the **concept** or the **things of which it is true**. Something counted as a genus in a predication may not, for that reason alone, be a real constituent of the external object. In particular, *not stipulating that something must accompany it* differs from stipulating that nothing accompanies it. The commentary continues into I:74 with a qualification about what may count as a genuine constituent.","prior_arabic_anchors_stage77":"«من حيث المفهوم متنافية ومن حيث الصدق أعم منها» · «أن جعل غير المبهم من أقسام هذه الماهية» · «فمعنى قول المصنف في شرح المقاصد أن معنى ذلك على ما من كون الأول أعم من الثاني» · «لا بشرط أن يكون معه شيء إنما يكون جنسا محمولا».","source_visual_check":"original Vol I p.73 (combined physical PDF p.86); same-editor image comparison; uncertain words remain","uncertain_readings":["Last clause begins فالمأخوذة لا بشرط أن يكون معه شيء; syntactic scope of أن requires second proof","The closing sentence should be reconciled with the continuation of lower discussion on I:74"]},{"id":"I074.lower.01","volume":1,"page":74,"physical_pdf_page":87,"layer":"lower","title":"The end of the I:73 classification, and the danger of moving between senses","arabic_anchors":"«واتحاده معا فلو اعتبر فيه التغاير فقط أو الاتحاد فقط كان الأول جزءا والثاني نوعا لا جنسا» · «وظاهر عبارة الشارح» · «لم يخف أنه استثناء من جميع ما سبق أن الماهية سواء على القول المتصور أو على اصطلاح الشيخ».","english":"—[The relevant meaning admits] both difference and identity together. If only **difference** were considered it would be a **part**; if only **identity** were considered it would be a **species**, not a genus. The outward wording of the commentator must therefore be read with the different senses distinguished. His expression does not by itself provide an unambiguous textual decision between the classifications previously discussed. Further, the earlier division of quiddity—whether according to the familiar classification or the Chief Master's terminology—divides it **as considered**, not as though the divisions were independently existing external quiddities. In the actual case one must still ask how the considered meaning is related to a thing's unity and its individuating features.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I074.png","source_image_sha256":"e032096c2236f62227dfb9320424c87b50d0abd7e561f9fc43b7bb6b01bf7b84","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":[],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I074.lower.02","volume":1,"page":74,"physical_pdf_page":87,"layer":"lower","title":"What “no parts” means and why the simple cannot be a hidden composite","arabic_anchors":"«(قوله لا جزء لها) أي في حد ذاتها» · «(قوله تقابلها) أشار به إلى أن البساطة والتركيب بهذا التفسير متنافيان» · «أن البساطة والتركيب بهذا التفسير متنافيان لا يصدقان على شيء» · «وقد يؤخذان متضايفين».","english":"On “It has no part”: that is, **in its own definition or essence**. On the discussion of opposition: the expressions **simplicity** and **composition**, under one sense of the definitions given, are mutually exclusive and cannot both be true of one and the same thing in the same respect. Yet the terms can also be used **relationally**. A thing may be simple relative to one analysis and composite relative to another. One must therefore prevent an inference that assumes they are absolutely contradictory while employing them in a comparative sense.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I074.png","source_image_sha256":"e032096c2236f62227dfb9320424c87b50d0abd7e561f9fc43b7bb6b01bf7b84","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":[],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I074.lower.03","volume":1,"page":74,"physical_pdf_page":87,"layer":"lower","title":"Conceptual parts versus real constituents","arabic_anchors":"«قد يوخذ البسيط بمعنى ما لا جزء له بالقياس إلى ما هو مركب منه» · «وقد يؤخذ المركب بمعنى جزء المركب والمركب بمعنى الكل» · «وبهذا التقدير لا يمنع من صدقهما» · «(قوله لأن الواحد مبدأ المتعدد الخ) أراد من الواحد والمتعدد».","english":"The simple may be taken in a restricted sense as **what is not a part of the relevant kind**, or as what does not have a part relative to a proposed composite. The composite in turn can be described in terms of its own component and the whole into which that component enters. Under a comparative interpretation some predicates of simplicity and composition can apply without inconsistency, so one cannot argue from the bare words to a contradiction. On “For unity is the principle of multiplicity”: what matters is the distinction between **the one and the many in the order being considered**. It should not be inferred without proof that an unrestricted unity is a real, separate constituent in every enumerated collection. The marginal argument on I:75 continues this issue by examining the attempted regress from multiplicity to unity.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I074.png","source_image_sha256":"e032096c2236f62227dfb9320424c87b50d0abd7e561f9fc43b7bb6b01bf7b84","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":["I075.lower.01."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I074.upper.01","volume":1,"page":74,"physical_pdf_page":87,"layer":"upper","title":"Real composition implies simplicity, without a regress of actual parts","arabic_anchors":"«لا جزء لها كالنقطة» · «وأما كيفية تقابلها كالجسم» · «ولا يخفى وجود الماهية المركبة ويلزم منه وجود البسيطة» · «(ولا بد من الانتهاء إلى البسيطة)» · «لأن الواحد مبدأ المتعدد».","english":"—[A simple quiddity] has no parts, as in the case of a point; other examples of what stands opposed to it are treated according to the kind of composition meant, such as body. The existence of **composite quiddities** is not in dispute, and the existence of composites entails that there must also be **simple quiddities**. “There must be an end at something simple.” For unity is the principle of multiplicity: if every one were itself a multitude of parts, whether finite or infinite, and every supposed constituent were in turn divisible without terminus, the account of an actually existing composite would fail to provide the basic unities on which the composition depends. The point is not merely that the intellect can divide a concept, but that the analysis of an actual composite cannot proceed without end in the way alleged.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I074.png","source_image_sha256":"e032096c2236f62227dfb9320424c87b50d0abd7e561f9fc43b7bb6b01bf7b84","stage":72,"attribution":"Taftazani embedded lemmas within Sanandaji commentary","notes":["I072.upper.01, not I073: the entire intervening I:73 is printed marginal commentary."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I074.upper.02","volume":1,"page":74,"physical_pdf_page":87,"layer":"upper","title":"Mutual dependence of the constituents of a real composite","arabic_anchors":"«(واحتياج بعض الأجزاء إلى بعض في المركب الحقيقي ضروري)» · «إذ لو استغنى كل من الأجزاء عن الآخر لم يحصل منها».","english":"“In a real composite it is necessary that some of its parts depend on others.” If each component were independent of every other one, no **single genuine compound** would arise from their aggregation. The argument proceeds on I:75 by distinguishing unity produced by a real form from the merely conceived unity of an aggregate.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I074.png","source_image_sha256":"e032096c2236f62227dfb9320424c87b50d0abd7e561f9fc43b7bb6b01bf7b84","stage":72,"attribution":"Taftazani embedded lemmas within Sanandaji commentary","notes":["I075.upper.01."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I075.lower.01","volume":1,"page":75,"physical_pdf_page":88,"layer":"lower","title":"Substrate, multiplicity, and a contested regress","arabic_anchors":"«أن يوجد فيه وحدات ويلزم منه انتهاء المعروض المتعدد إلى المعروض الواحد» · «لكن لا يلزم الانتهاء إلى البسيط بمعنى ما لا جزء له» · «بل جواز أن يكون ذلك المعروض الواحد مركبا» · «فتلزم كون العوارض بسائط بالمعنى المذكور ولكنه ليس هو المدعى».","english":"If there is a multiplicity of unities in a subject, it is argued that an underlying plurality must ultimately terminate at a **single bearer** of those predications. The marginal commentator concedes that this may establish some kind of unity, but denies that it necessarily establishes an **absolutely simple thing having no parts**. The single bearer might itself be composite under a different analysis. That would mean only that the accidents considered in relation to it are simple under the restricted sense just given, not that the bearer is simple in the absolute sense intended by the proof. The inference therefore moves from *a unified subject* to *an indivisible quiddity* without sufficient justification.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I075.png","source_image_sha256":"8e85205c65e50d66e222fb3d6606e02cda4155ab116c845fd0cfc8ac8b4592b6","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":[],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I075.lower.02","volume":1,"page":75,"physical_pdf_page":88,"layer":"lower","title":"The mistaken derivation of simplicity from intellectual composition","arabic_anchors":"«وقد يستدل على انتهاء المركب العقلي إلى البسيط بأنه لو لم ينته إليه لامتنع تعقل الماهية» · «لامتناع إحاطة العقل بما لا يتناهى» · «فإن معنى المركب العقلي هو ما لا يكون تمايز أجزائه إلا بحسب العقل».","english":"Another argument says that intellectual compounds must terminate in something simple; otherwise the intellect could not grasp a quiddity, since it cannot encompass an infinite number of constituents. The objection is that this misconstrues **intellectual composition**. Its parts are distinguished *by the intellect's analysis*, and it does not follow that the intellect must successively traverse an actually infinite multitude of separately real things in order to apprehend the whole. The conclusion would require an additional demonstration that the distinction of conceptual constituents implies a corresponding multiplicity which must be completed in actual cognition.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I075.png","source_image_sha256":"8e85205c65e50d66e222fb3d6606e02cda4155ab116c845fd0cfc8ac8b4592b6","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":[],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I075.lower.03","volume":1,"page":75,"physical_pdf_page":88,"layer":"lower","title":"Unity of real composition, and a warning about “material” parts","arabic_anchors":"«(قوله وحدة حقيقية الخ) للقطع بأنه لا يحصل من الحجر الموضوع بجنب الإنسان مثلا واحد حقيقي» · «(قوله من الجانبين) أي لكن لا باعتبار واحد وإلا لزم الدور» · «(قوله كذا قالوا) إشارة إلى أن مبناه على قواعدهم» · «(قوله قلنا الصورة الاجتماعية) حاصل الجواب».","english":"On “A genuine unity”: there can plainly be no genuine single being made merely by placing a stone beside a human being. On “On both sides”: the dependence must not be asserted **under a single and identical respect**, since that would yield circularity. On “Thus they say”: the expression reports the philosophers' principles rather than presenting them as a demonstration without remaining questions. On “We reply: the collective form”: the answer is that one must distinguish the **constitution of a real composite** from an intellectual consideration of togetherness. If “dependence” is said to concern the material constituents of genuine compounds, the claim is not automatically true of every aggregation of physical things. What needs proving is dependence among the **really existing constituents and the form** that makes them one. The next lower gloss takes up this point and qualifies the relation to the producer.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I075.png","source_image_sha256":"8e85205c65e50d66e222fb3d6606e02cda4155ab116c845fd0cfc8ac8b4592b6","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":["I076.lower.01."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I075.upper.01","volume":1,"page":75,"physical_pdf_page":88,"layer":"upper","title":"Genuine unity and asymmetric dependence between parts","arabic_anchors":"«ماهية واحدة ووحدة حقيقية» · «ثم الاحتياج فيما بين الجزأين قد يكون من جانب واحد كالتركيب من البسائط العنصرية» · «وعاما قد يكون من الصور المعدنية والنباتية والحيوانية إذ الصورة تحتاج إلى تلك المواد من غير عكس» · «وقد يكون من الجانبين كاحتياج الهيولى إلى الصورة وهن احتياج الصورة إلى الهيولى».","english":"—[Otherwise the components would not give] one quiddity and a genuine unity. Dependence between two constituents can sometimes run **one way only**, as in a compound of elemental simples. The forms involved in minerals, plants, and animals require their material substrata, without the opposite dependence necessarily following in the same sense. Or dependence may run **both ways**, as with prime matter and form: matter requires form, while form too requires matter in the relevant kind of corporeal composition. These examples distinguish the relation of ontological dependence from mere juxtaposition.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I075.png","source_image_sha256":"8e85205c65e50d66e222fb3d6606e02cda4155ab116c845fd0cfc8ac8b4592b6","stage":72,"attribution":"Taftazani embedded lemmas within Sanandaji commentary","notes":[],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I075.upper.02","volume":1,"page":75,"physical_pdf_page":88,"layer":"upper","title":"Why the collective form of an army is not another produced thing","arabic_anchors":"«(بخلاف المركب الاعتباري)» · «فإن قيل احتياج الهيئة الاجتماعية إلى الأجزاء المادية لازم قطعًا» · «قلنا الصورة الاجتماعية في المركبات الاعتبارية محض اعتبار العقل».","english":"“Unlike a considerational composite.” If it is objected that the **collective configuration** surely depends upon the material components that constitute it, we reply that the collective form of a merely considerational composite is **nothing but a consideration of the intellect**. It is not established as an additional extramental entity needing a cause in the same manner as a substantial form. This is why dependence of a genuine compound cannot be inferred directly from the need of an army for its soldiers or the need of an aggregation for its members. The next page develops the point explicitly through the army example.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I075.png","source_image_sha256":"8e85205c65e50d66e222fb3d6606e02cda4155ab116c845fd0cfc8ac8b4592b6","stage":72,"attribution":"Taftazani embedded lemmas within Sanandaji commentary","notes":["I076.upper.01."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I076.lower.01","volume":1,"page":76,"physical_pdf_page":89,"layer":"lower","title":"The lower commentator objects to how the debate is framed","arabic_anchors":"«الاجتماعية في المركب الاعتباري ليست موجودة في الخارج» · «مثل الترياق والسكنجبين مركبا حقيقيا» · «فإنه يحدث بتركيبه صورة في نفس الأمر» · «المركب الحقيقي من الجوهر والعرض» · «كالنفس والبدن للإنسان» · «(قوله إن المراد من المجعولية الخ)».","english":"The collective character of a **considerational composite** is not present externally. But if someone asks how a **real composite** is possible, consider a compound medicine such as *tiryāq* or *sakanjabīn*: through the composition, a form really arises and the components require that form as a principle or source. Even if the emergent form is granted to be a substantial form, the result does not by itself settle the special case of a composite constructed of accidents. One can only say that a real composite of substance and accident is possible if its constitutive relation is established—not that every aggregation must have the status of a genuine unity. The note also directs us to examples of genuinely existing constituent parts: soul and body in a human being; or such oppositions as necessity of existence and impossibility of nonexistence, which must not be confused with merely verbal enumeration. On “What is meant by made-ness, etc.” a previous author/commentator's account is introduced, and its full continuation is on I:77.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I076.png","source_image_sha256":"bf4956e04525e209cfa1a3bf5ba0643f2e06398357e0c9d1f9563526b551005e","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":["A short expression following the medicinal examples is compressed in the scan. The translation conveys the explicit comparison but does not introduce an unprinted substantial form as an established conclusion. The marginal stream continues across I076→I077."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I076.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":76,"physical_pdf_page":89,"layer":"ruled","title":"A separately ruled correction to the reading of “concomitant of existence”","arabic_anchors":"«(قوله من لوازم الوجود الخ) يعني ليس مراد المخالف أن الصادر عن الفاعل هو الوجود دون الماهية».","english":"On “Among the concomitants of existence, etc.” What the opponent means is **not** that what issues from the agent is existence without quiddity, as though existence were an external, self-subsisting entity and quiddity wholly excluded from the act of production. Existence is a matter of consideration, needing no agent considered as an independent entity. Rather, the point is that what is produced is quiddity **on condition that it is mixed with existence**. Under that description it requires an agent; it does not follow that the unqualified quiddity, considered apart from existence, likewise requires one. The dispute may thus turn on the *wording* or on which consideration of quiddity is intended. [The printed attribution/signature at the close is a reference to a prior discussion, not a securely identified individual author.]","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I076.png","source_image_sha256":"bf4956e04525e209cfa1a3bf5ba0643f2e06398357e0c9d1f9563526b551005e","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned separately ruled note","notes":["This note is bounded by two horizontal rules. Do not conflate it with either the upper sharḥ or the long lower ḥāshiya."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I076.upper.01","volume":1,"page":76,"physical_pdf_page":89,"layer":"upper","title":"On what can be externally real, and the status of collective unity","arabic_anchors":"«لا تحقق لها في الخارج إذ ليس من العسكر في الخارج إلا تلك الأفراد» · «ولو جعلت الهيئة جزءا من العسكر لم يكن العسكر أمرا موجودا في الخارج لأن ما جزؤه عدمي فهو عدمي» · «ثم اعلم أنهم اختلفوا في الماهيات الممكنة أهي مجعولة الجاعل أم لا».","english":"—There is no realization of it in the external world, for what exists externally of an army is nothing beyond its individual soldiers. If the collective configuration is made a constituent of the army, the army would no longer be an externally existing thing, since a thing one of whose constituents is non-existent is itself non-existent. Know, then, that they have differed about possible quiddities: are they things produced by an agent, or not? This is another disputed question, and the author has chosen to express it in terms of whether the possible thing needs an agent, for that is one of the concomitants of [being] a quiddity or of [the way it is treated as] produced. The difference in formulation must not be flattened into the claim that the author has already determined the issue.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I076.png","source_image_sha256":"bf4956e04525e209cfa1a3bf5ba0643f2e06398357e0c9d1f9563526b551005e","stage":72,"attribution":"Taftazani embedded lemmas within Sanandaji commentary","notes":["incoming I075.upper.final. The boundary between the last sentence about the collective form and the heading on possible quiddities is a transition in the printed main argument."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I076.upper.02","volume":1,"page":76,"physical_pdf_page":89,"layer":"upper","title":"What is meant by the claim that quiddity is not made","arabic_anchors":"«(ومن ثالث في مجعولية الماهية)» · «فقال بمجعولية الماهية أراد أنها تعرض للماهية في الجملة» · «ومن ثالث في مجعولية الماهية» · «(أراد أنها) أي المجعولية (من لوازم الوجود) وعوارض الهوية (كتناهي الأجسام) فإنه ليس».","english":"[The text] introduces a third disagreement about the *made-ness* (*majʿūliyya*) of a quiddity. The position denying that quiddity is produced concerns whether made-ness is attributed to quiddity as such, or whether it belongs to the quiddity only under a certain consideration. In explaining what was intended by the expression, the discussion identifies made-ness with the concomitants of existence and the accidents of concrete actuality, as in the finitude of bodies. The comparison extends into I:77; it should not be read as an argument that bodies themselves, or their quiddities considered in isolation, are independently created as distinct additional entities.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I076.png","source_image_sha256":"bf4956e04525e209cfa1a3bf5ba0643f2e06398357e0c9d1f9563526b551005e","stage":72,"attribution":"Taftazani embedded lemmas within Sanandaji commentary","notes":["The compressed typography in the clause before «أراد أنها» requires a separate line-level proof. The English here preserves the reported issue but is **interpretive at that exact point**, not a certified literal rendering.","outgoing I077.upper.01."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I077.lower.01","volume":1,"page":77,"physical_pdf_page":90,"layer":"lower","title":"Whether the need is for an agent or for something other than the composite","arabic_anchors":"«إن المجعولية قد يراد بها الاحتياج إلى الفاعل وقد يراد بها الاحتياج إلى الغير على ما يعم الجزء أيضا» · «وكلاهما من عوارض الممكن» · «الأول من لوازم هويته مطلقا والثاني من لوازم ماهية المركب دون البسيط».","english":"“Made-ness” may mean dependence on a producing agent; it may also mean dependence on something **other than itself**, in a sense broad enough to include dependence on a constituent part. Both are attributes of the possible thing. The first, however, is a concomitant of its concrete actuality, while the second pertains to the quiddity of a composite rather than that of a simple thing. Thus the word “made” is liable to equivocation unless the kind of dependence is determined.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I077.png","source_image_sha256":"a687c951fba1059a81c04d3adf3bf81dabd229f8188f7c314a2be83b93180219","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":[],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I077.lower.02","volume":1,"page":77,"physical_pdf_page":90,"layer":"lower","title":"Qualification of the alleged merely verbal dispute","arabic_anchors":"«(قوله فعلى ما ذكر يكون الخلاف لفظيا)» · «أقول هذه المسألة ... كما أشار إليه الشارح» · «لا تكاد تنضبط مذاهب المتكلمين» · «الماهيات بسيطة كانت أو مركبة تجعل الجاعل» · «جمهور الفلاسفة إلى أنها ليست بجعل الجاعل مطلقا».","english":"On “On what has been stated, the disagreement would be merely verbal”: I say that the question is not so easily brought under a single formula. The positions and terms used by the theologians are not readily reduced to one pattern. Some hold that quiddities, whether simple or composite, are produced by an agent, even where this requires acknowledging the production of a composite without separately producing its parts. The majority of philosophers are reported to hold that quiddities as such are not products of an agent in any unqualified sense; others distinguish simple from composite quiddities or disagree over whether a composite needs a producer by virtue of its parts. The commentator is warning that the seemingly verbal disagreement overlays several distinct propositions.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I077.png","source_image_sha256":"a687c951fba1059a81c04d3adf3bf81dabd229f8188f7c314a2be83b93180219","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":["The marginal enumeration and attribution of positions must be checked against the full preceding and following notes before assigning a view to a named school."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I077.lower.03","volume":1,"page":77,"physical_pdf_page":90,"layer":"lower","title":"The objection about an independently produced quiddity","arabic_anchors":"«فإن معنى المركب العقلي هو ما لا يكون تمايز أجزائه إلا بحسب العقل» · «ولا يستلزم كونه معقولا بأجزائه» · «لو كان جعل الجاعل لزم في تقدير عدم الجاعل أن لا يكون الإنسان إنسانا» · «فإنه عند ارتفاع الجاعل يرتفع الموجود وتبقى الماهية معدومة».","english":"The point is not answered by identifying an intellectual composite with a mere aggregate of separately existing things. A composite in the intellect is one whose components are differentiated by the intellect, and this need not entail separate actual existence for every intelligible constituent. The objector then says: if a producer makes the very quiddity of man, the removal of that producer would imply that a human being is no longer human. Two forms of the objection are considered. If this is a negative proposition, we do not concede that the quiddity of a human remains a subject about which that negative assertion could be made; the existent is removed with the producer, and the quiddity is left without external existence. If the claim is understood as one about the intelligible essence independently of the act of existing, the answer differs. The argument continues at the opening of I:78.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I077.png","source_image_sha256":"a687c951fba1059a81c04d3adf3bf81dabd229f8188f7c314a2be83b93180219","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":["outgoing I078.lower.01."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I077.upper.01","volume":1,"page":77,"physical_pdf_page":90,"layer":"upper","title":"Possible quiddities and the four-element example","arabic_anchors":"«من لوازم ماهية الجسم حتى لو تصورنا جسما غير متناه كان جسما» · «(لا من لوازم الماهية كزوجية الأربعة)» · «فإنها من لوازم ماهية الأربعة حتى إذا تصورنا الأربعة ليست بزوج لم تكن أربعة» · «(وإلا) يكن مراد المخالف ما ذكر لكن (كان احتياج الممكن إلى العلة ضروريا)».","english":"—[Bodily finitude belongs among the concomitants of the existence of body,] not the concomitants of the very quiddity of body: even if we imagined an infinite body, it would still be a body. “It is not like the evenness of four,” which belongs to the quiddity of four: if four were conceived as not even, it would not be four. “Otherwise”—if the opponent did not mean the distinction just mentioned, and instead denied that possible quiddities require an agent even under the relevant existential consideration—“the possible thing’s need of a cause would be necessary.” On the author's clarification, that would render the dispute only verbal; further investigation of the reality of existence and quiddity is therefore postponed to its proper place.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I077.png","source_image_sha256":"a687c951fba1059a81c04d3adf3bf81dabd229f8188f7c314a2be83b93180219","stage":72,"attribution":"Taftazani embedded lemmas within Sanandaji commentary","notes":["incoming I076.upper.02, outgoing I078.upper.01 (new chapter on individuation)."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I078.lower.01","volume":1,"page":78,"physical_pdf_page":91,"layer":"lower","title":"Completion of the I:77 objection","arabic_anchors":"«عند ارتفاع الجاعل لا يبقى الإنسان حتى يصح موضوعا للإيجاب» · «كذا في شرح المقاصد» · «وثانيا أن الوجود أو الاتصاف ماهية من الماهيات في القول بمجعوليتهما اعتراف بمجعوليته».","english":"—When the producer is removed, there is no human being left to serve as the subject of an affirmative proposition. Such is the discussion in *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*, and it deserves examination. Secondly, existence itself, or the qualification of a thing by existence, is a quiddity among quiddities if treated as an intelligible object; to affirm that its existence or its qualification is produced is thus to concede the production of a quiddity [under that consideration]. One must therefore attend to what exactly is asserted when the debate is called verbal.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I078.png","source_image_sha256":"e0809b9fade10449751e2fcd18ff66b3d75a855761c09308bd286533b7acac70","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":["incoming I077.lower.03."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I078.lower.02","volume":1,"page":78,"physical_pdf_page":91,"layer":"lower","title":"Existence and determination: the disagreement is not just an argument over words","arabic_anchors":"«فلا نزاع حقيقة وفيه أيضا نظر فإن النزاع في الماهيات التي هي حقائق الأشياء» · «لا فيما صدقت الماهية عليه من الأفراد» · «أكثر أدلة المتكلمين على تقدير تمامها أيضا لا تفيد إلا كون الوجود» · «بل اتصاف الماهية به من الفاعل في الحقيقة».","english":"But there is a further difficulty. The actual controversy concerns **quiddities insofar as they are the realities of things**, not simply the individual things of which a quiddity is predicated. Most of the proofs offered by the theologians, even supposing them to be complete, would establish only that existence—or the attribution of existence to a quiddity—comes from an efficient agent. They do not, simply as they stand, show that the quiddity in its own right is also produced. The individual expressions can be verbally reinterpreted while the metaphysical issue remains unresolved.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I078.png","source_image_sha256":"e0809b9fade10449751e2fcd18ff66b3d75a855761c09308bd286533b7acac70","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":[],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I078.lower.03","volume":1,"page":78,"physical_pdf_page":91,"layer":"lower","title":"What “shared” can mean in the claim about universals","arabic_anchors":"«(قوله مشتركا بينه وبين غيره)» · «فإن المراد من اشتراك تلك الأمور أما اشتراك كليها بين الأشياء» · «ولكن منع عدم اشتراك التعين كذلك» · «وأما اشتراك أفرادها» · «فممنوع ضرورة أن الفرد الموجود من الوحدة مثلا في هذا الشيء غير الفرد الموجود في ذلك الشيء».","english":"On “Shared between it and another.” The intended sharing may refer to the **universal notions** of these things being shared among particulars. But that would not establish that individuation as a universal notion is incapable of being shared—indeed, the text itself admits that it is a universal concept. Alternatively, the intended sharing may refer to **individual instances** of existence, unity, and quiddity. That reading is denied: the individual unity existing in this thing is not numerically the same individual unity existing in another. On either interpretation, the asserted contrast needs further clarification.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I078.png","source_image_sha256":"e0809b9fade10449751e2fcd18ff66b3d75a855761c09308bd286533b7acac70","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":[],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I078.lower.04","volume":1,"page":78,"physical_pdf_page":91,"layer":"lower","title":"Individual unity and a generic universal: the force of the objection","arabic_anchors":"«فإن كل شيء نوع منحصر في فرده» · «(قوله ولذا يصدق قولنا الكلي)» · «فإن عدم صدق قولنا الكلي متعين لا يقتضي عدم اشتراك التعين الذي بينه وبين غيره» · «إلا أن يشار بكلمة الإشارة إلى تعاير تلك الأمور».","english":"More strictly, each thing can be considered as belonging to its own determinate kind under the relation in question; it is not established that all the individual marks of one thing coincide with those of another. On the sentence “Therefore we can say of the universal...,” the failure of “the universal is individuated” to be true does **not** entail that there is no shared *concept* of individuation predicable in discussing that universal and others. Perhaps the contrast becomes defensible only if the expression is being used demonstratively, to point to **this particular** individuation rather than the common notion of individuation. The commentator therefore challenges the proposed inference, rather than simply repeating it.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I078.png","source_image_sha256":"e0809b9fade10449751e2fcd18ff66b3d75a855761c09308bd286533b7acac70","stage":72,"attribution":"unassigned lower printed commentary","notes":["The last phrase continues beyond the bottom of I:78 into I:79’s lower margin; its exact syntactic scope requires a joined proof. No unprinted conclusion is supplied."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I078.upper.01","volume":1,"page":78,"physical_pdf_page":91,"layer":"upper","title":"Chapter on individuation (*taʿayyun*)","arabic_anchors":"«فصل في التعين» · «تعين الشيء كما في شرح المقاصد غير ماهيته ووجوده ووحدته» · «لتكون كل من هذه الأمور مشتركا بينه وبين غيره بخلاف التعين» · «ولذا صدق قولنا الكلي ماهية وهو موجود وواحد» · «ولا يصدق أنه متعين وإن كان التعين مفهوما كليا».","english":"**Chapter on individuation.** The individuation of a thing, as stated in *Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid*, is other than its quiddity, its existence, and its unity. Each of those—quiddity, existence, and unity—can be shared in some way by a thing and another thing; **individuation**, by contrast, is what prevents that shared predication. Thus we can truthfully say that a universal has a quiddity, is existent [under the relevant sense of existence], and is one, but we cannot predicate individuation of the universal as such—even though *individuation* is itself a universal concept, as explained by what follows.","arabic":null,"arabic_status":"selected_source_anchors_only_not_diplomatic_full_text","reading_status":"first_pass_connected_interpretive_some_literal_checks_pending","source_image":"source_images/I078.png","source_image_sha256":"e0809b9fade10449751e2fcd18ff66b3d75a855761c09308bd286533b7acac70","stage":72,"attribution":"Taftazani embedded lemmas within Sanandaji commentary","notes":["outgoing I079.upper.01 (“as applicable to many...”). The top register on I:78 ends mid-sentence."],"source_kind":"locating_arabic_anchors_only","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I079.lower.01","volume":1,"page":79,"physical_pdf_page":92,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"التغاير فتدبر فالأولى هو ما في شرح المقاصد (قوله عموم من وجه) ولعل ذلك مبني على أنهم يريدون من التعين كون الشيء بحيث يصير مانعا من حمله على كثيرين ومن التميز كون الشيء بحيث ينفرد عن مشاركاته فالمعتبر في الأول كونه جزئيا سواء كان مع اعتبار المشاركة أو لا وفي الثاني التمايز عن المشارك سواء كان في الجزئي أو لا","english":"—difference. Reflect: the preferable account is therefore that in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid. On “Overlap without either wholly including the other.” This may rest on their meaning by determination that a thing is such as to prevent its predication of many, and by distinction that it is set apart from things sharing with it. What matters in the first is its being particular, whether or not sharing is considered; in the second, its being distinguished from something sharing with it, whether or not the distinction occurs at the level of a particular.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I079.png","source_sha256":"210a6ce7210341067b6603619b8e38f85ef17d76a93b90e634a74875f06a347a","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"join_in":"I078.lower.final","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I079.lower.02","volume":1,"page":79,"physical_pdf_page":92,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله في أن التعين وجودي أو عدمي الخ) قد يقال أمر اعتباري لا تحقق له في الأعيان إذ لو كان موجودا في الخارج كان له تعين وهكذا فيتسلسل وقد يجاب بأنه إنما يلزم ذلك لو كانت التعينات متشاركة في الماهية لتحتاج في التمايز إلى التعين لم لا يجوز أن تكون مختلفة الماهية متمايزة بالذات وإنما تتشارك في لفظ التعين أو في عرضي لها هو مفهوم التعين","english":"On “Whether determination is existential or privative, etc.” It may be said that determination is a matter of consideration, with no realization among external things: if it existed externally, it would have a determination of its own, and so on without end. The reply may be that this follows only if determinations share a quiddity, requiring another determination to distinguish them. Why could they not have different quiddities and be intrinsically distinct, sharing only the word “determination” or an accidental feature, namely the concept of determination?","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I079.png","source_sha256":"210a6ce7210341067b6603619b8e38f85ef17d76a93b90e634a74875f06a347a","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I079.lower.03","volume":1,"page":79,"physical_pdf_page":92,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وأما حديث كون تعين التعين نفسه فقد قيل إنه مدفوع بأن ذلك الاتحاد إنما يصح في الأمور الاعتبارية كالحدوث ونحوه وأما التعين فهو من الأمور الخارجية العارضة للعين في الخارج وتغاير العارض والمعروض الخارجيين ضروري كذا في شرح المقاصد لكن فيه تأمل لا يخفى كما سيأتي","english":"As for the claim that a determination’s determination is itself, it has been said that this is refuted by the fact that such identity is valid only in matters of consideration, such as origination and the like. Determination, by contrast, is an external thing attaching to an entity externally, and an external attribute and its external bearer are necessarily different. This is what is stated in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid. But it plainly calls for examination, as will become clear.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I079.png","source_sha256":"210a6ce7210341067b6603619b8e38f85ef17d76a93b90e634a74875f06a347a","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I079.lower.04","volume":1,"page":79,"physical_pdf_page":92,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وقد يقال إنه وجودي لأنه جزء المتعين إذ هو عبارة عن الماهية والتعين وهو موجود وجزء الموجود موجود ورد بأنه لم لا يجوز أن يكون المتعين هو نحو الإنسان المقيد بالعوارض المشخصة دون المجموع ولو سلم فهم لا يسلمون كون تلك العوارض الموجودة تعينا بل ما به التعين","english":"It may also be said that determination is existential because it is a part of the determined thing, which consists of quiddity and determination. The determined thing exists, and a part of what exists also exists. The objection is: why could the determined thing not be, for example, the human being qualified by individuating accidents, rather than the aggregate [of that subject and those accidents]? Even if this is granted, they do not concede that those existing accidents are determination itself; rather, they are that through which determination occurs.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I079.png","source_sha256":"210a6ce7210341067b6603619b8e38f85ef17d76a93b90e634a74875f06a347a","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I079.upper.01","volume":1,"page":79,"physical_pdf_page":92,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"صادقا على الكثرة وبين التعين والتميز عموم من وجه لصدقهما على مشخصات الأفراد إذ المعتبر مشاركتها في الماهية مثلا فإن كلا منها متشخص في نفسه ومتميز عن غيره وصدق التعين دون التميز حيث لا تعتبر المشاركة وبالعكس على تميز الكليات كالأنواع المعتبر اشتراكها في الجنس هذا ثم اعلم أنه اختلف في أن التعين وجودي أو عدمي حقيقي أو اعتباري فلا بد في تحقيق أنه ما هو من بيان ما هو المراد من هذه الألفاظ فنقول (أفراد النوع إما تتمايز بعوارض ربما تنتهي) تلك العوارض","english":"—as applying to many. Determination and distinction overlap without either wholly including the other. Both apply to the individuating features of individuals when, for example, their sharing in a quiddity is taken into account: each is individuated in itself and distinct from the other. Determination applies without distinction where sharing is not taken into account. Conversely, distinction applies without determination to the distinction between universals, such as species considered as sharing in a genus. Having established this, know that there is disagreement over whether determination is existential or privative, real or merely a matter of consideration. To establish what it is, we must therefore explain what these expressions mean. We say: “The individuals of a species are either distinguished by accidents which may terminate”—those accidents may terminate—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I079.png","source_sha256":"210a6ce7210341067b6603619b8e38f85ef17d76a93b90e634a74875f06a347a","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"join_in":"I078.upper.final","join_out":"I080.upper.01","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I080.lower.01","volume":1,"page":80,"physical_pdf_page":93,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله إلى ما يفيد الهاذية الخ) لعله أراد بما يفيدها كون الفرد بحيث لا يقبل الشركة (قوله وامتناع الشركة) عطف تفسير للهاذية (قوله أي العوارض نفسها) تفسير ما لا تفسير ضمير المفعول فإنه للهاذية وتخصيص ما يفيد بالعوارض مع أنها مفيد بعيد إنما لتصريح بعده بالمفيد القريب أعني كون الفرد بحيث لا يقبل الشركة فتدبر","english":"On “In what produces thisness, etc.” Perhaps by what produces it he means the individual’s being such that it does not admit sharing. On “And the impossibility of sharing.” This is an explanatory coordination clarifying “thisness.” On “Namely the accidents themselves.” This explains “what,” not the object pronoun, which refers to thisness. “What produces it” is specified as the accidents, although they are a remote producer, only because the proximate producer is explicitly stated afterward: the individual’s being such that it does not admit sharing. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I080.png","source_sha256":"8ad16f1896e8b139b0c7f8fc3acd4104c850478ab65544ef080e7f0466ab5862","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I080.lower.02","volume":1,"page":80,"physical_pdf_page":93,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أي أن يشترك فيه) أي معنى التعين هو كون الفرد بحيث يمتنع أن يشترك فيه أفراد أي يمتنع أن يحمل على كثيرين لا كونه بحيث لا يشترك مع غيره في شيء إذ لا متعين من إلا وهو له شريك في أمر كلي فافهم","english":"On “That several things share in it.” The meaning of determination is that the individual is such that several individuals cannot share in it—that is, it cannot be predicated of many. It does not mean that it shares nothing with anything else, for every determinate thing has something else sharing with it in a universal feature. Understand.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I080.png","source_sha256":"8ad16f1896e8b139b0c7f8fc3acd4104c850478ab65544ef080e7f0466ab5862","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"readings":[{"locus":"إذ لا متعين من إلا","status":"The small word من between متعين and إلا appears extraneous or damaged. The clear assertion is that every determinate thing shares some universal feature; no missing Arabic is supplied."}],"all_source_words_resolved":false,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I080.lower.03","volume":1,"page":80,"physical_pdf_page":93,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله المركب مع وجودي الخ) أي المعتبر على طريق العدم والملكة (قوله أو غير المركب) أي المعتبر على طريق الإيجاب والسلب","english":"On “Compounded with an existential element, etc.” That is, considered in terms of privation and possession. On “Or uncompounded.” That is, considered in terms of affirmation and negation.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I080.png","source_sha256":"8ad16f1896e8b139b0c7f8fc3acd4104c850478ab65544ef080e7f0466ab5862","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I080.upper.01","volume":1,"page":80,"physical_pdf_page":93,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(إلى ما يفيد الهاذية) وامتناع الشركة كذا إذا كانت الأفراد من الموجودات الخارجية (فبعد تلخيص أن) المراد من (التعين والتشخص هو تلك الهاذية) الحاصلة بالعوارض (أو ما يفيدها) أي العوارض نفسها (أو كون الفرد بحيث لا يقبل الشركة) أي أن يشترك فيه (أو عدم قبوله لها) أي للشركة","english":"—“in what produces thisness” (hādhiyya) and the impossibility of sharing. This applies when the individuals are external existents. “After clarifying that” what is meant by “determination and individuation is that thisness” produced by the accidents, “or what produces it,” namely the accidents themselves, “or the individual’s being such that it does not admit sharing”—that is, that several things share in it—“or its not admitting it,” namely sharing—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I080.png","source_sha256":"8ad16f1896e8b139b0c7f8fc3acd4104c850478ab65544ef080e7f0466ab5862","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"join_in":"I079.upper.01","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I080.upper.02","volume":1,"page":80,"physical_pdf_page":93,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) بعد تلخيص (أن) المراد بـ (العدمي هو العدم أو العدم) المطلق أو (المضاف) المركب مع وجودي كعدم البصر عما من شأنه أن يكون بصيرا أو غير المركب كعدم قبول الشركة (أو ما يدخل في مفهومه العدم) ككون الشيء بحيث لا يقبل الشركة (و) أن المراد من (الوجودي) ما كان (بخلافه) فهو الموجود أو الوجود مطلقا أو المضاف أو ما لا يدخل في مفهومه العدم والعبرة بالمعنى حتى إن العمى عدمي وإن اللاعمى وجودي","english":"“And” after clarifying “that” what is meant by “privative is nonexistence, or nonexistence”—unrestricted or “relative,” compounded with an existential element, as with the absence of sight in what is capable of seeing; or uncompounded, as with not admitting sharing—“or that whose concept includes nonexistence,” as with a thing’s being such that it does not admit sharing; “and” that the “existential” is what is “opposed to it”: an existent, existence whether unrestricted or relative, or that whose concept does not include nonexistence. Meaning is what matters: blindness is privative, while non-blindness is existential.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I080.png","source_sha256":"8ad16f1896e8b139b0c7f8fc3acd4104c850478ab65544ef080e7f0466ab5862","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I080.upper.03","volume":1,"page":80,"physical_pdf_page":93,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) بعد تلخيص (أن) المراد من (الحقيقي ما له ثبوت في نفس الأمر) أي في حد ذاته (من غير شائبة فرض وتقدير) من العقل (و) أن المراد من (الاعتباري) ما كان (بخلافه) يعني ما لا تحقق له إلا بحسب فرض العقل (لا يشتبه أن التعين وجودي أو عدمي حقيقي أو اعتباري) فلا خفاء في أن العوارض وجودية والهاذية اعتبارية وكون الفرد بحيث لا يقبل الشركة أو عدم قبوله لها عدميان (و) أيضا (لا يشتبه) عليك (أنه) أي التعين لا يحصل بانضمام","english":"“And” after clarifying “that” the “real is what is established in the thing itself”—in its own right—“without any admixture of supposition or positing” by the intellect, “and” that the “considerational” is what is “opposed to it,” meaning what has no realization except through intellectual supposition, “there is no ambiguity as to whether determination is existential or privative, real or considerational.” It is clear that the accidents are existential; thisness is considerational; and the individual’s being such that it does not admit sharing, or its not admitting sharing, are both privative. “And” it is also “not unclear” to you “that it”—determination—does not arise by adding—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I080.png","source_sha256":"8ad16f1896e8b139b0c7f8fc3acd4104c850478ab65544ef080e7f0466ab5862","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"join_out":"I081.upper.01","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I081.lower.01","volume":1,"page":81,"physical_pdf_page":94,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لأن كلا من المنضم والمنضم إليه الخ) إن قيل حكم الكل قد يخالف حكم كل فيجوز أن يكون كل من المنضم والمنضم إليه كليا والمجموع جزئيا قلنا لا معنى للانضمام هاهنا سوى أن العقل يعتبر مفهوما كليا ثم يعتبر له وصفا كليا ومعلوم بالضرورة أن الكلي الموصوف بالأوصاف الكلية لا ينتهي إلى حد المادية وإن كان ذلك الوصف هو مفهوم الجزئية وامتناع قبول الشركة أو يقال مرادهم من نفي حصول التعين من ذلك الانضمام هو أن انضمام الكلي إلى الكلي لا يستلزم التعين والتشخص لا أنه لا يفيده أصلا فالمقصود أن المعنى الذي بسببه امتنع للعقل فرض صدق المفهوم على كثيرين لا بد أن يستند إلى أمر يستلزمه","english":"On “For both what is added and what it is added to, etc.” If it is said that the whole may have a judgment different from that of each component, so that both components may be universal while the whole is particular, we reply: addition here means only that the intellect considers a universal concept and then considers a universal attribute of it. It is necessarily known that a universal qualified by universal attributes does not thereby reach [the limit designated by a word read provisionally as “materiality”], even when the attribute is the concept of particularity or of the impossibility of sharing. Alternatively, one may say that, in denying that determination arises from such addition, their intention is that adding one universal to another does not entail determination and individuation: not that it never produces them at all. The intended point is that the factor because of which the intellect cannot suppose a concept to apply to many must depend on something that entails that factor.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I081.png","source_sha256":"2cbc47c50e5d016464a1bb183ab71606218e074f17a99965e97d06067c7b730e","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"readings":[{"locus":"إلى حد المادية","status":"The noun is provisionally read المادية (materiality). The argument concerns particularity; no conjectural replacement has been inserted."}],"proof_notes":["I081_lower_second.png resolves لا أنه: lack of entailment is not a denial of every instance of production."],"all_source_words_resolved":false,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I081.lower.02","volume":1,"page":81,"physical_pdf_page":94,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قال فهو إما مستند إلى الفاعل القادر الخ) فهو الموجد مستندا لكل ما شاء من التشخصات على ما سيأتي تفصيله (قوله لا يفيد العلية) أي فيجوز أن الوجود ما معه التعين لا ما هو علة التعين","english":"He says, “It therefore depends either on a powerful agent, etc.” He is the originator, serving as the ground of whichever individuations He wills, as will be explained in detail. On “Does not establish causation.” That is, existence may be that with which determination occurs, rather than that which causes determination.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I081.png","source_sha256":"2cbc47c50e5d016464a1bb183ab71606218e074f17a99965e97d06067c7b730e","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"readings":[{"locus":"فهو الموجد مستندا","status":"The printed grammatical construction is compressed; the attribution of individuation to the originating agent is retained without silently regularizing the Arabic."}],"all_source_words_resolved":false,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I081.paratext.01","volume":1,"page":81,"physical_pdf_page":94,"layer":"paratext","attribution":"printer paratext","arabic":"(٦ ـ تقريب أول)","english":"[Printing/signature mark:] “6 — Taqrīb, first [part].”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I081.png","source_sha256":"2cbc47c50e5d016464a1bb183ab71606218e074f17a99965e97d06067c7b730e","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"editorial":"A signature mark beneath the text frame, not an additional philosophical argument.","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I081.upper.01","volume":1,"page":81,"physical_pdf_page":94,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"الكلي إلى الكلي لأن كلا من المنضم والمنضم إليه يمكن للعقل فرض صدقه على كثيرين والتعين على كل تقدير لا بد فيه من كون المفهوم بحيث لا يمكن للعقل ذلك (فهو إما مستند إلى الفاعل القادر) المختار كغيره من الممكنات على ما هو عندنا من استناد الكل إليه تعالى (أو إلى الوجود الخارجي) عند بعض للقطع بأن الماهية إذا تحققت في الخارج لا تقبل الشركة وإنما تقبلها في العقل","english":"—one universal to another. For the intellect can suppose both what is added and what it is added to as applying to many. Determination, on any account, requires the concept to be such that the intellect cannot do that. “It therefore depends either on a powerful agent” acting by choice, as other possible things do, according to our position that everything depends on Him, exalted is He, “or on external existence,” according to some. For we are certain that a quiddity, when realized externally, does not admit sharing; it admits sharing only in the intellect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I081.png","source_sha256":"2cbc47c50e5d016464a1bb183ab71606218e074f17a99965e97d06067c7b730e","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"join_in":"I080.upper.03","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I081.upper.02","volume":1,"page":81,"physical_pdf_page":94,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"فإن قيل فيلزم أن لا يتعدد التعين لأن الوجود أمر واحد ومقتضى الواحد لا يكون إلا واحدا أجيب أن الوجود وإن كان بحسب المفهوم واحدا لكن تتعدد أفراده بحسب الأزمنة والأمكنة وسائر الأسباب وبعد اعترض بأن الدوران لا يفيد العلية فإن قيل نحن نقطع بالعلية عند الوجود الخارجي مع قطع النظر عن جميع ما عداه","english":"If it is objected that determination would then not be multiple, because existence is one thing and what one thing entails can only be one, the answer is that existence, although one in concept, has multiple instances according to times, places and other causes. A further objection is that concomitant occurrence does not establish causation. If it is said, “We are certain of causation when external existence is present, without considering anything besides it”—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I081.png","source_sha256":"2cbc47c50e5d016464a1bb183ab71606218e074f17a99965e97d06067c7b730e","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"join_out":"I082.upper.01","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I082.lower.01","volume":1,"page":82,"physical_pdf_page":95,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لا يوجب انتفاءه) وبالجملة عند الوجود لا بد من أمر يستند إليه الوجود فيجوز أن يستند إليه التعين أيضا ولو سلم فالوجود لا يقتضي إلا تعينا ما والكلام في التعينات المخصوصة فلا يثبت المطلوب اللهم إلا أن وجود كل فرد يقتضي تعينه الخاص","english":"On “Does not entail its absence.” In sum, when existence obtains, there must be something on which it depends. Determination may also depend on that same thing. Even if the premise is granted, existence entails only some determination, whereas the discussion concerns specific determinations; the desired conclusion is therefore not established—unless it is said that the existence of each individual entails its own particular determination.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I082.png","source_sha256":"b6c0466ae59058fb0ed5b3dff2205f36b589cfffbef56ecebd48473c397f1518","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I082.lower.02","volume":1,"page":82,"physical_pdf_page":95,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كما ذهب إليه الفلاسفة الخ) حاصل ما ذهبوا إليه على ما يستفاد من شرح المقاصد هو أن الموجود إما مادي أو غيره والثاني إما واجب كالباري أو ممكن وهو العقل والأول إما مادي في ذاته كالأجسام والأعراض أو مادي في التعلق كالنفوس البشرية والفلكية فغير المادي بقسميه تعينه مستند إلى نفس ماهيته فتنحصر كل ماهية في فرد لكن العقول لما كانت مختلفة الماهيات متعددة عندهم تعدد أفرادها وكل فرد منها لنوع من الماهية انحصرت فيه بخلاف الواجب فإن ماهيته لما لم تكن متعددة بل واحدة انحصرت في فرد واحد ولم يتصور هناك تعدد أصلا لا بحسب الفرد ولا بحسب النوع والمادي بنوعيه تعينه مستند إلى المادة أي محله والمراد به كما فصله بعضهم المعروض في الأعراض والمادة في الأجسام والمتعلق في النفوس بناء على ما ذكروا من حدوث النفس بعد البدن وتعينها به","english":"On “As the philosophers hold, etc.” The substance of their position, as understood from Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, is this. An existent is either material or otherwise. The latter is either necessary, like the Creator, or possible, namely an intellect. The former is either material in itself, like bodies and accidents, or material in its attachment, like human and celestial souls. Both kinds of non-material existent derive determination from their own quiddity, so each quiddity is confined to one individual. But since the intellects, on their account, have diverse and multiple quiddities, their individuals are multiple; each individual belongs to a species of quiddity confined to it. The Necessary Existent is different: since His quiddity is not multiple but one, it is confined to a single individual, and no multiplicity can be conceived there, whether of individuals or of species. Both kinds of material existent derive their determination from matter, that is, their subject. As some have explained in detail, this means the bearer in the case of accidents, matter in bodies, and the object of attachment in souls. The last rests on their claim that the soul comes into being after the body and is determined by it.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I082.png","source_sha256":"b6c0466ae59058fb0ed5b3dff2205f36b589cfffbef56ecebd48473c397f1518","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I082.lower.03","volume":1,"page":82,"physical_pdf_page":95,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"إذا عرفت هذا عرفت أن في تحرير الشارح «مد ظله» نوع اضطراب فإن قوله فينحصر نوعها في الشخص الواحد إلى قوله هذا إذا كان تعين الماهية زائدا عليها الخ لبيان استناد تعين المجردات كالعقول إلى ماهيتها فحينئذ إما أن يريد بقوله وأما إذا كانت الماهية متعينة في ذاتها الخ بيان أن ماهية الواجب مغايرة ماهية المجردات في أن تعينها ليس معللا بها زائدا عليها بل هو نفسها كما هو الظاهر من السياق مع أنه","english":"Once you understand this, you will see a certain inconsistency in the commentator’s formulation—may his protection endure. His words from “Its species is then confined to the single individual” to “This applies when the quiddity’s determination is additional to it, etc.” explain how the determination of separate entities, such as intellects, depends on their quiddities. His subsequent words, “But when the quiddity is determined in its very essence, etc.,” must then intend either to explain that the Necessary Existent’s quiddity differs from that of separate entities, in that its determination is not additional to it and caused by it, but is the quiddity itself, as the context appears to suggest—although that—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I082.png","source_sha256":"b6c0466ae59058fb0ed5b3dff2205f36b589cfffbef56ecebd48473c397f1518","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"join_out":"I083.lower.01","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I082.upper.01","volume":1,"page":82,"physical_pdf_page":95,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"قلنا قطع النظر عن الشيء لا يوجب انتفاءه (أو إلى أسباب أخر) كما ذهب إليه الفلاسفة من أن التعين قد يستند إلى (نفس الماهية) فينحصر نوعها في الشخص الواحد الحاصل من الماهية والتعين الذي علل بها لم يمكن أن يوجد معها تعين آخر وإلا انفك عنها التعين الأول فيتخلف المعلول عن علته هذا إذا كان تعين الماهية زائدا عليها معللا بها وأما إذا كانت الماهية متعينة في ذاتها ممتنعة في نفسها عن فرض الاشتراك فيها كالواجب تعالى على رأيهم فلا يتصور هناك تعدد أصلا","english":"We reply: not considering something does not entail its absence. “Or [determination depends] on other causes,” as the philosophers hold. Determination may depend on “the quiddity itself.” Its species is then confined to the single individual consisting of the quiddity and the determination caused by it. No other determination can exist along with that quiddity; otherwise the first determination would be separated from it, and the effect would fail to accompany its cause. This applies when the quiddity’s determination is additional to it and caused by it. But when the quiddity is determined in its very essence, itself precluding the supposition of being shared—as with the Necessary Existent, exalted is He, on their account—no multiplicity can be conceived there at all.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I082.png","source_sha256":"b6c0466ae59058fb0ed5b3dff2205f36b589cfffbef56ecebd48473c397f1518","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"join_in":"I081.upper.02","readings":[{"locus":"الذي علل بها لم يمكن","status":"The print runs the clauses together. Punctuation in the translation distinguishes the single individual from the subsequent denial of another determination; no Arabic words are supplied."}],"all_source_words_resolved":false,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I083.lower.01","volume":1,"page":83,"physical_pdf_page":96,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"خلاف ما ذهبوا إليه هناك كما مر آنفا يرد عليه أنه مع القول زيادة تعينها عليها وكونه معللا بها لم يتم القول بأنه لا يتصور هناك تعدد لا فردا ولا نوعا كما سبق إلا أن يقال المراد أنه حينئذ لا يتصور تعدد لا ذهنا ولا خارجا على ما صرح به المصنف في شرح المقاصد من أن قولهم أن لكل موجود ماهية كلية في العقل وإن امتنع تعدد أفرادها في الخارج مخصوص بغير الواجب تعالى فإنه في حقه محل نظر فتأمل","english":"—is contrary to their position in that discussion, as just explained. The objection to him is that, while maintaining that its determination is additional to it and caused by it, the assertion that no multiplicity can be conceived there, whether individual or specific, does not follow in the manner stated—unless the intention is that multiplicity cannot then be conceived either mentally or externally. The author explicitly states in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid that their claim, “Every existent has a universal quiddity in the intellect, even if it cannot have multiple external individuals,” applies only to what is other than the Necessary Existent, exalted is He; in His case, it is open to examination. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I083.png","source_sha256":"3ac5f31d0959cc3976a1b62c78e043914d970a946bb90039bbcea327d9549f2b","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"join_in":"I082.lower.03","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I083.lower.02","volume":1,"page":83,"physical_pdf_page":96,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وإما أن يريد به بيان أنها معادلة ماهية المجردات في امتناع تعدد أفرادها مطلقا وإن كان تعينها معللا بها أيضا كان حق العبارة أن يقول هذا إذا كانت الماهية مختلفة متنوعة وأما إذا كانت متحدة كالواجب تعالى فلا يتصور هناك الخ فتفطن فإن الإمساك من تحقيق أمثال هذا المقام ليس بسبيل الكرام","english":"Or he intends to explain that it is on a par with the quiddity of separate entities in the absolute impossibility of multiple individuals, even if its determination is likewise caused by it. In that case the formulation should have been: “This applies when the quiddity is diverse and differentiated into species. But when it is one, as with the Necessary Existent, exalted is He, no multiplicity can be conceived there,” and so forth. Attend carefully: refraining from investigating matters of this kind is not the way of noble scholars.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I083.png","source_sha256":"3ac5f31d0959cc3976a1b62c78e043914d970a946bb90039bbcea327d9549f2b","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I083.lower.03","volume":1,"page":83,"physical_pdf_page":96,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله من العوارض المتعينة في نفسها الخ) إشارة إلى جواب ما اعترض عليه من أن تعين الأعراض الحالة في المادة إنما هو بتعين المادة على ما سيجيء فلو تعينت المادة بها كان دورا وحاصل الجواب أن تعين المادة إنما هو بنفس الأعراض المتعينة بنفسها تعينا لا بتعيناتها المحلية الحاصلة بتعين المادة فلا دور ولا حصول التشخص بانضمام الكلي إلا أنه يرد أنه إذا جاز ذلك فلم لا يجوز تكثير الماهية وتعين أفرادها بما يلحقها من الأمور العارضة من غير لزوم مادة كذا قيل","english":"On “By the accidents that are determinate in themselves, etc.” This points to the answer to an objection: accidents inhering in matter derive their determination from the determination of matter, as will be explained; were matter determined by them, circularity would follow. The substance of the answer is that matter is determined by the accidents themselves, which possess determination in themselves, not by their determinations as localized in a subject, which result from the determination of matter. There is therefore no circularity, nor does individuation arise by adding a universal. Nevertheless, the objection arises: if this is possible, why should a quiddity not be multiplied and its individuals determined through adventitious factors without requiring matter? So it has been said.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I083.png","source_sha256":"3ac5f31d0959cc3976a1b62c78e043914d970a946bb90039bbcea327d9549f2b","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"readings":[{"locus":"لا بتعيناتها المحلية","status":"The adjective read as المحلية is clear enough for a provisional rendering concerning localized determination, but remains a locus for comparison with a clearer witness."}],"all_source_words_resolved":false,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I083.upper.01","volume":1,"page":83,"physical_pdf_page":96,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(أو إلى المادة المتشخصة) إما بالذات كهيولات الأفلاك فإن هيولى كل فلك متشخصة في نفسها وممتازة عن هيولى فلك آخر وإما (بما يلحقها من العوارض) المتعينة في نفسها اللاحقة (بحسب تعاقب الاستعدادات) كهيولى العناصر الأربعة فإنها واحدة مشتركة بينها تشخصها وتعددها بما يعرض تلحقها بحسب تعاقب استعدادات مختلفة بحسب القرب والبعد من الفلك","english":"“Or [determination depends] on individuated matter”: either matter individuated intrinsically, like the prime matters of the celestial spheres—for each sphere’s prime matter is individuated in itself and distinct from that of another sphere—or matter individuated “by the accidents that attach to it,” themselves determinate and attaching “according to successive dispositions.” An example is the prime matter of the four elements: it is one and shared among them; its individuation and multiplication arise through what attaches to it according to the succession of different dispositions, depending on proximity to, or distance from, the celestial sphere.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I083.png","source_sha256":"3ac5f31d0959cc3976a1b62c78e043914d970a946bb90039bbcea327d9549f2b","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"readings":[{"locus":"بما يعرض تلحقها","status":"The scan seems to have a compressed or faulty phrase. The translation gives the relation of adventitious accidents to matter, without silently restoring a new Arabic wording."}],"all_source_words_resolved":false,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I084.lower.01","volume":1,"page":84,"physical_pdf_page":97,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله هي من الأمور العارضة للماهية الخ) قيل جعل الامتناع من لواحق الوجود والماهية إنما هو بالنظر إلى أن ضرورة سلب الوجود عن الماهية حال لهما أو إلى أنه من أوصاف الماهية المعقولة أو لكونه في مقابلة الإمكان","english":"On “These are things that attach to a quiddity, etc.” It has been said that impossibility is counted among the concomitants of existence and quiddity either because the necessity of denying existence of a quiddity is a condition of the two; because it is an attribute of the conceived quiddity; or because it stands in opposition to possibility.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I084.png","source_sha256":"72fc4b003407808550b34f603f8370c7fbc19520e0370ed55c49f774b507c9d8","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I084.lower.02","volume":1,"page":84,"physical_pdf_page":97,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فإذا نسب المفهوم إلى وجوده الخ) أي إذا حمل الوجود في نفسه أو لأمر على مفهوم سواء بطريق الإيجاب أو السلب حصلت تلك المعاني فلا يرد أنها تحصل عند حمل العدم أو الربط بواسطته كما تحصل عند حمل الوجود كذلك وذلك لاندراجه فيما ذكرناه كما مر جوابه","english":"On “When a concept is related to its existence, etc.” That is, when existence, whether in itself or for something, is predicated of a concept, affirmatively or negatively, those meanings arise. It is therefore no objection that they arise when nonexistence is predicated or used as the connecting term just as they arise when existence is predicated in that way. Such cases are included in what we have stated, as the answer has already explained.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I084.png","source_sha256":"72fc4b003407808550b34f603f8370c7fbc19520e0370ed55c49f774b507c9d8","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I084.upper.01","volume":1,"page":84,"physical_pdf_page":97,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"فصل في بيان (الوجوب والامتناع والإمكان) هي من الأمور العارضة للماهية بالقياس إلى الوجود كالقدم والحدوث كما سيجيء إن شاء الله تعالى وهي (معقولات) أي معان عقلية (تحصل من نسبة المفهوم إلى هلية بسيطة) أي وجود في نفسه كأن يقال هل العنقاء موجود (أو مركبة) أي وجوده لأمر كأن يقال هل الحركة دائمة","english":"Chapter explaining “necessity, impossibility and possibility.” These are things that attach to a quiddity in relation to existence, like eternity and origination, as will be explained, God willing. They are “intelligibles”—that is, intellectual meanings—“arising from relating a concept to a simple existential proposition,” namely existence in itself, as when one asks, “Does the phoenix exist?” “Or to a compound existential proposition,” namely its existence for something, as when one asks, “Is motion everlasting?”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I084.png","source_sha256":"72fc4b003407808550b34f603f8370c7fbc19520e0370ed55c49f774b507c9d8","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I084.upper.02","volume":1,"page":84,"physical_pdf_page":97,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"قال في شرح المقاصد إن هل إما بسيطة يطلب بها الوجود الذي في نفسه أو مركبة يطلب بها وجود شيء لشيء فإذا نسب المفهوم إلى وجوده في نفسه أو وجوده لأمر حصل في العقل معان هي الوجوب والامتناع والإمكان لأن حمل الوجود على الشيء أو ربط الشيء بالشيء بواسطة الوجود إما على وجه الوجوب كقولنا صانع العالم موجود والأربعة يوجد لها الزوجية أو على وجه الامتناع كقولنا شريك الباري موجود والأربعة يوجد لها الفردية وإما على وجه الإمكان كقولنا الإنسان موجود أو يوجد له الكتابة ⟦والمغايرة لهما في هلية كل ما في كذا؟⟧","english":"He says in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid that a question with “whether” (hal) is either simple, asking about existence in itself, or compound, asking about one thing’s existence for another. When a concept is related to its existence in itself or its existence for something, intellectual meanings arise: necessity, impossibility and possibility. For existence is predicated of a thing, or one thing is connected with another by means of existence, either necessarily—as in “The Maker of the world exists” and “Evenness belongs to four”; impossibly—as in “A partner to the Creator exists” and “Oddness belongs to four”; or possibly—as in “A human being exists” or “Writing belongs to a human being.” [The final printed phrase concerning difference and existential predication is not securely read.]","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I084.png","source_sha256":"72fc4b003407808550b34f603f8370c7fbc19520e0370ed55c49f774b507c9d8","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"readings":[{"locus":"والمغايرة لهما في هلية كل ما في كذا","status":"Unresolved short printed ending. The Arabic inside editorial brackets is an insecure reading, not established source wording. A targeted parallel-text web search did not locate a usable match; no external source is substituted."}],"all_source_words_resolved":false,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I084.upper.03","volume":1,"page":84,"physical_pdf_page":97,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) هذه الأمور الثلاثة (تصورها ضروري) لا يحتاج إلى التعريف (والتعريف بمثل ضرورة الوجود) للوجوب (أو ضرورة العدم) للامتناع","english":"“And” these three things are such that “their conception is self-evident,” requiring no definition. “Definition by something like the necessity of existence,” for necessity, “or the necessity of nonexistence,” for impossibility—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I084.png","source_sha256":"72fc4b003407808550b34f603f8370c7fbc19520e0370ed55c49f774b507c9d8","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"join_out":"I085.upper.01","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I085.lower.01","volume":1,"page":85,"physical_pdf_page":98,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله يقصد به تفسير مدلول اللفظ الخ) ولهذا لا يتحاشى من أن يقال الواجب ما يمتنع عدمه والممتنع ما يجب عدمه والممكن ما لا يجب وجوده ولا عدمه ولا يمتنع وجوده ولا عدمه ولو كان القصد إلى إفادة تصور هذه المعاني لكان دورا ظاهرا","english":"On “Its purpose is to explain what the expression signifies, etc.” For this reason one does not avoid saying: the necessary is that whose nonexistence is impossible; the impossible is that whose nonexistence is necessary; and the possible is that whose existence and nonexistence are neither necessary nor impossible. Had the purpose been to impart a conception of these meanings, the circularity would have been evident.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I085.png","source_sha256":"86168efc464f49c81f4271444e7f052adb56334d19f47aa86087e0ed0f85183b","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I085.lower.02","volume":1,"page":85,"physical_pdf_page":98,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وعدم اجتماع النقيضين) مثال الامتناع الذاتي الحاصل من النسبة إلى الهلية البسيطة كالمثال الذي سبق وسيأتي أعني شريك الباري وههنا بحث لم أر من تعرض له وهو أن الامتناع المعقول هاهنا حاصل من نسبة وجود الاجتماع للنقيضين لا لوجود الاجتماع في نفسه فإن الاجتماع ليس ممتنعا في نفسه بل الممتنع إنما هو وجوده للنقيضين وكذا الشريك ليس ممتنعا في نفسه بل الممتنع إنما هو وجوده للباري فهما كامتناع الفردية للأربعة في الحصول من النسبة إلى الهلية المركبة مع اتفاقهم على تمثيلهما للهلية البسيطة","english":"On “The non-occurrence of the conjunction of contradictories.” This is an example of intrinsic impossibility arising in relation to a simple existential proposition, like the earlier and forthcoming example of a partner to the Creator. There is a question here that I have not seen anyone address. The impossibility understood in this case arises from attributing conjunction to the contradictories, not from the existence of conjunction in itself. Conjunction is not impossible in itself; what is impossible is only its existence for the two contradictories. Likewise, a partner is not impossible in itself; what is impossible is its existence as a partner to the Creator. These two cases therefore resemble the impossibility of oddness in four: they arise from the relation expressed in a compound existential proposition. Yet the authors agree in using them to exemplify a simple existential proposition.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I085.png","source_sha256":"86168efc464f49c81f4271444e7f052adb56334d19f47aa86087e0ed0f85183b","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"proof_notes":["I085_lower1_proof.png resolves إنما هو; the earlier اتحاده was a reading error."],"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I085.lower.03","volume":1,"page":85,"physical_pdf_page":98,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"والجواب أن الامتناع لاجتماع النقيضين قد يعتبر بحيث يكون معقولا من الوجود المأخوذ رابطة بين الاجتماع والنقيضين كأن يقال هل للاجتماع وجود للنقيضين فحينئذ يكون مستفادا من الهلية المركبة وقد يعتبر بحيث يكون معقولا من الوجود المأخوذ محمولا كأن يقال هل اجتماع النقيضين موجود فيكون مستفادا من الهلية البسيطة وهذا هو المراد عند التمثيل ومورد محل حديث امتناع شريك الباري وغيره فتدبر فإنه دقيق ومزيد أيضا (قوله وإلا) أي فإن لم تكن ضرورة الوجود واللاوجود البسيطين أو المركبين بالنظر إلى ذات","english":"The answer is that the impossibility of the conjunction of contradictories may be considered as understood from existence taken as a copula between conjunction and the contradictories: as though one asked, “Does conjunction exist for the two contradictories?” It would then be obtained from a compound existential proposition. Alternatively, it may be considered as understood from existence taken as the predicate: as though one asked, “Does the conjunction of contradictories exist?” It would then be obtained from a simple existential proposition. The latter is what is intended when giving the example, and is the setting in which the impossibility of a partner to the Creator and similar cases is discussed. Reflect: it is subtle, and [the final evaluative expression is uncertain]. On “Otherwise”: that is, if the necessity of existence and nonexistence, whether simple or compound, is not considered in relation to the essence of—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I085.png","source_sha256":"86168efc464f49c81f4271444e7f052adb56334d19f47aa86087e0ed0f85183b","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"join_out":"I086.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"ومورد محل حديث ... ومزيد أيضا","status":"The short introductory construction and the evaluative wording before the next lemma need another reading; the two copular/predicative examples are transcribed in full."}],"proof_notes":["I085_lower2_proof.png shows معقولا, not مقولا, at both occurrences. The final brief evaluative expression remains open."],"all_source_words_resolved":false,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I085.upper.01","volume":1,"page":85,"physical_pdf_page":98,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(أو لا ضرورتهما) للإمكان (لفظي) يقصد به تفسير مدلول اللفظ وضرورة التصور أي المغني عن التعريف المعنوي (وينقسم كل من الأولين) يعني الوجوب والامتناع دون الأخير أعني الإمكان لأنه ذاتي ليس إلا وإلا لكان الشيء واجبا أو ممتنعا أي ضروري الوجود أو العدم بالذات ولا ضروري الوجود والعدم بالغير فيرتفع ما بالذات وهذا معنى الانقلاب (إلى الذاتي والغيري) لأن ضرورة وجود الشيء أو لا وجوده في نفسه أو ضرورة وجوده لشيء أو لا وجوده له إن كانت بالنظر إلى ذات الموضوع كوجود الباري وعدم اجتماع النقيضين ووجود الزوجية للأربعة وعدم الفردية لها فذاتي وإلا فغيري (فالموصوف بالذاتي من","english":"“Or the non-necessity of both,” as a definition of possibility, is “verbal”: its purpose is to explain what the expression signifies. The necessity of the conception means that it dispenses with a definition of the meaning. “Each of the first two”—necessity and impossibility, but not the last, namely possibility, since possibility is intrinsic only—“is divided into intrinsic and extrinsic.” Otherwise, a thing could be necessary or impossible, that is, necessarily existent or nonexistent intrinsically, yet neither necessarily existent nor necessarily nonexistent through another. What belongs to it intrinsically would then be removed: this is what a reversal [of its intrinsic modal status] means. The necessity of a thing’s existence or nonexistence in itself, or of its existence or nonexistence for something, is intrinsic when considered in relation to the subject’s own essence. Examples are the Creator’s existence, the non-occurrence of the conjunction of contradictories, the presence of evenness in four, and the absence of oddness from four. Otherwise it is extrinsic. “Thus that which is qualified as intrinsic among—”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I085.png","source_sha256":"86168efc464f49c81f4271444e7f052adb56334d19f47aa86087e0ed0f85183b","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"join_in":"I084.upper.final","join_out":"I086.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"وضرورة التصور أي المغني","status":"The construction is compressed. The scan is read as ضرورة التصور, followed by a gloss concerning dispensing with a meaning-definition; this should be checked again with I:84."}],"all_source_words_resolved":false,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I086.lower.01","volume":1,"page":86,"physical_pdf_page":99,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"الموضوع كوجود زيد مثلا حين وجوده وعدم بكر حين عدمه ووجود القيام لزيد كذلك وعدم القعود لبكر كذلك (قال واجب الوجود لذاته) يحتمل أن تكون اللام صلة الوجود للاختصاص لا للتعليل وكذا في المعطوف بقوله أو لشيء آخر فيكون المقصود إفادة تعميم الوجوب الذاتي على البسيط والمركب ويحتمل أن تكون اللام فيهما صلة الوجوب للتعليل فيكون المقصود إفادة التعميم مع التصريح بكون الوجوب فيهما ذاتيا فتأمل فإنه دقيق","english":"—the subject: for example, Zayd’s existence while he exists, Bakr’s nonexistence while he does not exist, the presence of standing in Zayd in the same way, and the absence of sitting from Bakr in the same way. He says, “Necessarily existent in itself.” The lām may qualify “existence,” expressing specific attribution rather than causation; the same applies to the coordinated expression “or for another thing.” The purpose would then be to extend intrinsic necessity to both simple and compound existential propositions. Alternatively, in both places the lām may qualify “necessity” causally; the purpose would then be that generalization together with an explicit statement that the necessity in both is intrinsic. Reflect, for this is subtle.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I086.png","source_sha256":"77606e6bc85aabfa17bdf4419c1fed14e124f18d5ff22653d4c71c4b4549e481","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":true,"independent_review":false,"join_in":"I085.lower.03","editorial":"The ruled note rejects a causal reading of the lām; the long lower gloss entertains it as an alternative. The disagreement is retained.","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":true,"proof_scope":"All visible upper/ruled/lower text on the page reread against original scan by the same assistant; adjacent joins checked. Not independent scholarly review or witness collation.","proof_notes":["Full page review: upper and any ruled section compared separately from lower commentary; all units on this page represented. An affirmative local editorial closure, not a claim of a definitive critical text."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I086.lower.02","volume":1,"page":86,"physical_pdf_page":99,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فإنها لازمة ماهية الأربعة وواجبة الوجود لها الخ) أي واجبة الثبوت للماهية الأربعية نظرا إلى نفس تلك الماهية لا واجبة الوجود لذات الزوجية بمعنى اقتضائها الوجود بالذات ليستلزم المحال قال في شرح المقاصد وبهذا سقط ما في المواقف من أنه يلزم كون لوازم الماهيات واجبة لذاتها وهو باطل وذلك لأنه إن أراد كونها واجبة لذات اللوازم فاللازمة ممنوعة أو لذات الماهيات فبطلان التالي ممنوع (قوله كممكن هو واجب الوجود) أي","english":"On “It is inseparable from the quiddity of four and necessarily belongs to it, etc.” That is, it necessarily holds of the quiddity of four by consideration of that very quiddity. It is not necessarily existent because of the essence of evenness, in the sense that evenness intrinsically requires existence, so as to entail an impossibility. He says in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid: “This disposes of the claim in al-Mawāqif that it follows that the concomitants of quiddities are necessary in themselves, which is false. For if what is meant is that they are necessary because of the essences of the concomitants, the entailment is denied; or, if it is because of the essences of the quiddities, the alleged falsity of the consequent is denied.” On “Like a possible thing that is necessarily existent”: that is—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I086.png","source_sha256":"77606e6bc85aabfa17bdf4419c1fed14e124f18d5ff22653d4c71c4b4549e481","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":true,"independent_review":false,"join_out":"I087.lower.01","proof_notes":["I086_lower_proof.png resolves ليستلزم المحال and the full quotation, including فاللازمة ممنوعة. No witness beyond the supplied print.","Full page review: upper and any ruled section compared separately from lower commentary; all units on this page represented. An affirmative local editorial closure, not a claim of a definitive critical text."],"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":true,"proof_scope":"All visible upper/ruled/lower text on the page reread against original scan by the same assistant; adjacent joins checked. Not independent scholarly review or witness collation.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I086.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":86,"physical_pdf_page":99,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لذاته) ليست اللام هنا وفي قوله لشيء آخر للتعليل فتكون متعلقة بالامتناع بل لام الإضافة تفيد إضافة الوجود إلى مدخولها اه منه «مد ظله»","english":"On “In itself” (li-dhātihi). The lām here, and in “for another thing” (li-shayʾ ākhar), is not causal, so as to qualify impossibility. Rather, it is the lām of attribution: it attributes existence to the expression following it. End. [Signed:] “From him”—“may his protection endure.”","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I086.png","source_sha256":"77606e6bc85aabfa17bdf4419c1fed14e124f18d5ff22653d4c71c4b4549e481","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":true,"independent_review":false,"editorial":"The signature منه and honorific are reproduced without assigning a personal author.","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":true,"proof_scope":"All visible upper/ruled/lower text on the page reread against original scan by the same assistant; adjacent joins checked. Not independent scholarly review or witness collation.","proof_notes":["Full page review: upper and any ruled section compared separately from lower commentary; all units on this page represented. An affirmative local editorial closure, not a claim of a definitive critical text."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I086.upper.01","volume":1,"page":86,"physical_pdf_page":99,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"الواجب) أي الموصوف بالوجوب بالذات (واجب الوجود لذاته) كما إذا أخذ الوجود محمولا (وهو الله تعالى أو) واجب الوجود (لشيء آخر) هو الموضوع كما إذا أخذ رابطة بين المحمول والموضوع (كزوجية الأربعة) فإنها لازمة ماهية الأربعة وواجبة الوجود لها بالنظر إلى نفسها من غير احتياج إلى أمر آخر","english":"—“the necessary,” that is, what is qualified by necessity intrinsically, “is necessarily existent in itself,” as when existence is taken as a predicate—“and this is God, exalted is He—or” necessarily existent “for another thing,” namely the subject, as when existence is taken as a copula between predicate and subject. An example is “the evenness of four”: it is inseparable from the quiddity of four and necessarily belongs to it when four is considered in itself, without need of anything else.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I086.png","source_sha256":"77606e6bc85aabfa17bdf4419c1fed14e124f18d5ff22653d4c71c4b4549e481","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":true,"independent_review":false,"join_in":"I085.upper.01","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":true,"proof_scope":"All visible upper/ruled/lower text on the page reread against original scan by the same assistant; adjacent joins checked. Not independent scholarly review or witness collation.","proof_notes":["Full page review: upper and any ruled section compared separately from lower commentary; all units on this page represented. An affirmative local editorial closure, not a claim of a definitive critical text."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I086.upper.02","volume":1,"page":86,"physical_pdf_page":99,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) الموصوف بالذاتي (من الممتنع) أي الموصوف بالامتناع الذاتي هو إما (ممتنع الوجود لذاته) كما إذا أخذ الوجود محمولا (كشريك الباري أو) ممتنع الوجود (لشيء آخر) هو الموضوع كما إذا أخذ رابطة بين الموضوع والمحمول (كفردية الأربعة والموصوف بالغيري) من الواجب (كممكن هو واجب الوجود","english":"“And” that which is intrinsically qualified “among the impossible”—that is, what is intrinsically impossible—is either “impossible in its own existence,” as when existence is taken as the predicate, “like a partner to the Creator,” or impossible in its existence “for another thing,” namely the subject, as when existence is taken as a copula between subject and predicate, “like the oddness of four. What is qualified as extrinsic” among the necessary is “like a possible thing that is necessarily existent—”","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I086.png","source_sha256":"77606e6bc85aabfa17bdf4419c1fed14e124f18d5ff22653d4c71c4b4549e481","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":true,"independent_review":false,"join_out":"I087.upper.01","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":true,"proof_scope":"All visible upper/ruled/lower text on the page reread against original scan by the same assistant; adjacent joins checked. Not independent scholarly review or witness collation.","proof_notes":["Full page review: upper and any ruled section compared separately from lower commentary; all units on this page represented. An affirmative local editorial closure, not a claim of a definitive critical text."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I087.lower.01","volume":1,"page":87,"physical_pdf_page":100,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"لذاته أو لشيء آخر وكذا قوله ممتنع الوجود (قوله حين وجوده وحين عدمه) أشار به إلى أن يكون الأول واجبا بالغير والثاني ممتنعا به إنما يكون إذا لم يكن الوجوب والامتناع فيهما لذات الموضوع فإنهما لما اعتبر كونهما حين الوجود والعدم للموضوع فهما لعلة أخرى غير ذات الموضوع فتأمل","english":"—in itself or for another thing; the same applies to his expression “impossible in existence.” On “While it exists” and “while it does not exist.” By this he indicates that the first is necessary through another and the second impossible through another only when the necessity and impossibility do not arise from the subject’s essence. Since the two are considered as obtaining at the time of the subject’s existence and nonexistence, they arise from a cause other than the subject’s essence. Reflect.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I087.png","source_sha256":"48ac8d6f74587cb87cd1f147e653710bc95f560e0730e5811e420d474bb706c9","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":true,"independent_review":false,"join_in":"I086.lower.02","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":true,"proof_scope":"All visible upper/ruled/lower text on the page reread against original scan by the same assistant; adjacent joins checked. Not independent scholarly review or witness collation.","proof_notes":["Full page review: upper and any ruled section compared separately from lower commentary; all units on this page represented. An affirmative local editorial closure, not a claim of a definitive critical text."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I087.lower.02","volume":1,"page":87,"physical_pdf_page":100,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله المقابل للوجوب والامتناع) وذلك لأن بينهما انفصالا حقيقيا لأن كل مفهوم إما أن يكون ضروري الوجود أو لا والثاني إما أن يكون ضروري العدم أو لا فالثلاثة لا تجتمع ولا ترتفع والاعتراض بضروري الوجود والعدم غير وارد لأنه مفهوم إذا لاحظه العقل لم يكن إلا ضروري العدم فافهم والتقييد لذاته أنه لا ينفك من الوجوب والامتناع بالغير كما سبق قال في شرح المقاصد الإمكان ينفك من الوجوب والامتناع بالغير لكن بحسب التعقل بأن لا يلاحظ الماهية ولا علتها وجودا أو عدما لا بحسب التحقق اه","english":"On “Opposed to necessity and impossibility.” This is because the division between them is a genuine exclusive and exhaustive disjunction. Every concept either has necessary existence or does not; in the latter case it either has necessary nonexistence or does not. The three therefore neither coexist nor all fail to obtain. The objection from something whose existence and nonexistence are both necessary does not apply, because, when the intellect considers that concept, it can only be necessarily nonexistent. Understand. The qualification “intrinsically” is used because possibility is not separated from necessity or impossibility through another, as explained previously. He says in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid: “Possibility can be separated from necessity and impossibility through another, but in conception: neither the quiddity nor its cause is considered as existent or nonexistent. It cannot be separated from them in actual realization.” End.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I087.png","source_sha256":"48ac8d6f74587cb87cd1f147e653710bc95f560e0730e5811e420d474bb706c9","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":true,"independent_review":false,"proof_notes":["I087_lower_proof.png resolves إلا. The passage treats an intrinsically contradictory concept as impossible, not as a fourth modal alternative.","Full page review: upper and any ruled section compared separately from lower commentary; all units on this page represented. An affirmative local editorial closure, not a claim of a definitive critical text."],"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":true,"proof_scope":"All visible upper/ruled/lower text on the page reread against original scan by the same assistant; adjacent joins checked. Not independent scholarly review or witness collation.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I087.lower.03","volume":1,"page":87,"physical_pdf_page":100,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"أقول لو أطلق الإمكان بمعنى لاضرورة الوجود والعدم بالغير أيضا حصل التقابل بينه وبين الوجوب والامتناع بالغير أيضا لكنه لما لم يتحقق له مصداق في نفس الأمر لم يعتبره كما مر فتأمله (قوله مع قطع النظر عن جانب العدم) أي لا مع النظر إلى ضرورة العدم فإنه لو نظر إلى ضرورة العدم مع سلب ضرورة الوجود انحصر في الامتناع وليس يفهم معنى الضرورة","english":"I say: were possibility also used to mean the non-necessity, through another, of existence and nonexistence, opposition would likewise obtain between it and necessity and impossibility through another. But since it has no instance in reality, he did not count it, as stated earlier. Consider this. On “Without considering the side of nonexistence.” That is, not while considering the necessity of nonexistence. If the necessity of nonexistence were considered together with the negation of the necessity of existence, the meaning would be restricted to impossibility. The meaning of necessity is not understood—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I087.png","source_sha256":"48ac8d6f74587cb87cd1f147e653710bc95f560e0730e5811e420d474bb706c9","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":true,"independent_review":false,"join_out":"I088.lower.01","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":true,"proof_scope":"All visible upper/ruled/lower text on the page reread against original scan by the same assistant; adjacent joins checked. Not independent scholarly review or witness collation.","proof_notes":["Full page review: upper and any ruled section compared separately from lower commentary; all units on this page represented. An affirmative local editorial closure, not a claim of a definitive critical text."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I087.upper.01","volume":1,"page":87,"physical_pdf_page":100,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"حين وجوده و) من الممتنع كممكن هو (ممتنع الوجود حين عدمه) ثم الإمكان بالمعنى المذكور هو الإمكان الخاص المقابل للوجوب والامتناع بالذات (وقد يؤخذ بمعنى سلب ضرورة الوجود) مع قطع النظر عن جانب العدم فيقابل الوجوب ويعم الإمكان الخاص والامتناع (أو) بمعنى سلب ضرورة (العدم) مع قطع النظر عن جانب الوجود فيقابل الامتناع ويعم الإمكان الخاص والوجوب (فيسمى بالإمكان العام لعمومه الخاص وضرورة الطرف الآخر وقد يعتبر)","english":"—“while it exists; and” among the impossible, like a possible thing that is “impossible in existence while it does not exist.” Possibility in the sense just stated is specific possibility, opposed to intrinsic necessity and intrinsic impossibility. “It may be taken to mean the negation of the necessity of existence,” without considering the side of nonexistence. It then opposes necessity and includes specific possibility and impossibility. “Or” it may mean the negation of the necessity “of nonexistence,” without considering the side of existence. It then opposes impossibility and includes specific possibility and necessity. “It is therefore called general possibility, since it includes specific possibility and the necessity of the other side. It may also be considered—”","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I087.png","source_sha256":"48ac8d6f74587cb87cd1f147e653710bc95f560e0730e5811e420d474bb706c9","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":true,"independent_review":false,"join_in":"I086.upper.02","join_out":"I088.upper.01","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":true,"proof_scope":"All visible upper/ruled/lower text on the page reread against original scan by the same assistant; adjacent joins checked. Not independent scholarly review or witness collation.","proof_notes":["Full page review: upper and any ruled section compared separately from lower commentary; all units on this page represented. An affirmative local editorial closure, not a claim of a definitive critical text."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I088.lower.01","volume":1,"page":88,"physical_pdf_page":101,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"من إطلاق لفظ الإمكان أصلا بل المفهوم منه إذا قيد بالعدم هو أن الوجود ليس بضروري أعم من أن يكون العدم غير ضروري أيضا كما في الإمكان الخاص أو ضروريا كما في الامتناع فإذا قيل إن الممتنع ممكن فمعناه هو أن وجوده ليس بضروري لأن عدمه ضروري غاية الأمر أن سلب ضرورة الوجود قد يصدق بما عدمه ضروري فتفطن","english":"—from an unrestricted use of the word “possibility” at all. Rather, when it is qualified by nonexistence, what is understood is that existence is not necessary. This is more general than whether nonexistence is also non-necessary, as in specific possibility, or necessary, as in impossibility. Thus, when one says that the impossible is possible, the meaning is that its existence is not necessary—because its nonexistence is necessary. At most, the negation of the necessity of existence can be true of something whose nonexistence is necessary. Attend carefully.","reading_status":"source_corrected_selected_locus_not_all_register_second_proof","source_image":"source_images/I088.png","source_sha256":"19ba4bf09b68bcc35f8a8dcee4b8fec0a6e0de2cb93dc180539e2d485f6b0fdd","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"join_in":"I087.lower.03","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"prior_Arabic_stage78":"من إطلاق لفظ الإمكان أصلا بل المفهوم منه إذا قيد بالعدم هو أن الوجود ليس بضروري أعم من أن يكون العدم غير ضروري أيضا كما في الإمكان الخاص أو ضروريا كما في الامتناع فإذا قيل إن الممتنع ممكن فمعناه هو أن وجوده ليس بضروري لا أن عدمه ضروري غاية الأمر أن سلب ضرورة الوجود قد يصدق بما عدمه ضروري فتفطن","prior_English_stage78":"—from an unrestricted use of the word “possibility” at all. Rather, when it is qualified by nonexistence, what is understood is that existence is not necessary. This is more general than whether nonexistence is also non-necessary, as in specific possibility, or necessary, as in impossibility. Thus, when one says that the impossible is possible, the meaning is that its existence is not necessary—not that its nonexistence is necessary. At most, the negation of the necessity of existence can be true of something whose nonexistence is necessary. Attend carefully.","source_visual_check":"Original I:88, lower text, first six lines: printed لأن عدمه ضروري, not لا أن عدمه ضروري. Verified directly with magnified source crop; logic reverses if misread.","critical_apparatus":[{"kind":"logical_proof_note","reading":"لأن عدمه ضروري","effect":"because its nonexistence is necessary; treating the first two letters as لا أن would negate and invert the argument about general possibility","basis":"Direct Būlāq I:88 plus paragraph context I:87–89; confirmed in Stage79","status":"locally_rechecked_not_independent"}],"stage81_same_editor_visual_review":"whole_visible_page_second_source_inspection_unresolved_glyphs_not_externally_certified","stage81_register_audit":"one upper line + continuous lower gloss","stage81_boundary_audit":"I:87 lower -> I:88 lower -> I:89 lower; upper I:88 -> I:89","stage81_proof_note":"Modal possibility: the negative is لأن عدمه ضروري, already corrected in Stage79. Printed contrast of broad/particular possibility preserved."},{"id":"I088.lower.02","volume":1,"page":88,"physical_pdf_page":101,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وكذا معنى قوله مع قطع النظر عن جانب الوجود أي لا مع النظر إلى ضرورة الوجود فإنه لو نظر إليها معه لانحصر في الوجوب وليس يطلق الإمكان عليه غايته أنه قد يطلق على معنى يصدق للوجوب أيضا والحاصل أن العامة يفهمون من إمكان وجود الشيء نفي امتناع وجوده ومن إمكان عدم الشيء نفي امتناع عدمه ولهذا يسمى بالإمكان العامي كما يسمى بالإمكان العام ولفهمهم هذا إطلاقه كذلك نفي الامتناع عنه قد يقع الممكن العام مقابلا للممتنع شاملا للواجب كما في تقسيمهم الكلي إلى الممتنع والممكن الذي أحد أقسامه ما يوجد منه فرد واحد مع امتناع غيره فافهم","english":"The same applies to his expression “without considering the side of existence”: that is, not while considering the necessity of existence. Were that necessity considered along with it, the meaning would be restricted to necessity, and possibility is not applied to necessity itself. At most, it is applied in a sense that is also true of what is necessary. In sum, ordinary people understand the possibility of a thing’s existence as the denial that its existence is impossible, and the possibility of its nonexistence as the denial that its nonexistence is impossible. Hence it is called ordinary possibility as well as general possibility. Because they understand its application in this way, as the denial of impossibility, the generally possible may be opposed to the impossible and include the necessary. This occurs in their division of the universal into impossible and possible: one subdivision of the latter has a single existing individual, with any other being impossible. Understand.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I088.png","source_sha256":"19ba4bf09b68bcc35f8a8dcee4b8fec0a6e0de2cb93dc180539e2d485f6b0fdd","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"readings":[{"locus":"ولفهمهم هذا إطلاقه كذلك نفي الامتناع عنه","status":"The connective construction is unusually compressed. The translation retains the clear point that ordinary usage understands possibility as denial of impossibility; the exact syntax remains open."}],"all_source_words_resolved":false,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"stage81_same_editor_visual_review":"whole_visible_page_second_source_inspection_unresolved_glyphs_not_externally_certified","stage81_register_audit":"one upper line + continuous lower gloss","stage81_boundary_audit":"I:87 lower -> I:88 lower -> I:89 lower; upper I:88 -> I:89","stage81_proof_note":"Modal possibility: the negative is لأن عدمه ضروري, already corrected in Stage79. Printed contrast of broad/particular possibility preserved."},{"id":"I088.lower.03","volume":1,"page":88,"physical_pdf_page":101,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"إذا سمعت هذا علمت أن ما نسب إلى بعض الأوهام من أن للإمكان العام مفهوما واحدا هو سلب الضرورة من أحد الطرفين على الإبهام بحيث يشمل الثلاثة أعني الإمكان الخاص والوجوب والامتناع بعيد جدا إذ لا يفهم هذا الأعم من إمكان الشيء على الإطلاق بل إنما يفهم أحد المفهومين من إمكان وجود الشيء والآخر من إمكان عدمه فتدبر","english":"Having heard this, you will know how far-fetched is the view ascribed to certain mistaken understandings: that general possibility has a single concept, namely the negation of necessity on one indeterminate side, so as to include all three—specific possibility, necessity and impossibility. This broader meaning is not understood simply from a thing’s being possible. Rather, one of the two meanings is understood from the possibility of its existence, and the other from the possibility of its nonexistence. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I088.png","source_sha256":"19ba4bf09b68bcc35f8a8dcee4b8fec0a6e0de2cb93dc180539e2d485f6b0fdd","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"stage81_same_editor_visual_review":"whole_visible_page_second_source_inspection_unresolved_glyphs_not_externally_certified","stage81_register_audit":"one upper line + continuous lower gloss","stage81_boundary_audit":"I:87 lower -> I:88 lower -> I:89 lower; upper I:88 -> I:89","stage81_proof_note":"Modal possibility: the negative is لأن عدمه ضروري, already corrected in Stage79. Printed contrast of broad/particular possibility preserved."},{"id":"I088.lower.04","volume":1,"page":88,"physical_pdf_page":101,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ولهذا قال في شرح المقاصد ما حاصله أنه بهذا ينحل ما يقال على قاعدة كون نقيض الأعم أخص من نقيض الأخص من أنه لو صح لصدق قولنا كل ما ليس بممكن عام ليس بممكن خاص لكنه باطل لأن كل ما ليس بممكن خاص إما واجب أو ممتنع وكل منهما ممكن عام فيلزم أن كل ما ليس بممكن خاص فهو ممكن عام وهذا إذا ضم للمقدمة الأولى أعني قولنا كل ما ليس بممكن عام ليس بممكن خاص أنتج كل ما ليس بممكن عام فهو ممكن عام وهو باطل لاستلزامه اجتماع النقيضين اه","english":"For this reason, the substance of what he says in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid is that this resolves the objection advanced against the rule that the contradictory of a more general predicate is more specific than the contradictory of a more specific predicate. Were the rule correct, it is said, the proposition “Everything that is not generally possible is not specifically possible” would be true. But that is false: everything that is not specifically possible is either necessary or impossible, and each is generally possible. It follows that everything that is not specifically possible is generally possible. Joined to the first premise—“Everything that is not generally possible is not specifically possible”—this yields: “Everything that is not generally possible is generally possible.” That is false because it entails the conjunction of contradictories. End.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I088.png","source_sha256":"19ba4bf09b68bcc35f8a8dcee4b8fec0a6e0de2cb93dc180539e2d485f6b0fdd","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"proof_notes":["I088_lower_end_proof.png resolves أنه بهذا ينحل ما يقال: the distinction resolves the objection rather than merely illustrating it."],"all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"stage81_same_editor_visual_review":"whole_visible_page_second_source_inspection_unresolved_glyphs_not_externally_certified","stage81_register_audit":"one upper line + continuous lower gloss","stage81_boundary_audit":"I:87 lower -> I:88 lower -> I:89 lower; upper I:88 -> I:89","stage81_proof_note":"Modal possibility: the negative is لأن عدمه ضروري, already corrected in Stage79. Printed contrast of broad/particular possibility preserved."},{"id":"I088.lower.05","volume":1,"page":88,"physical_pdf_page":101,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ووجه الاختلال ما مر من عدم كون مفهوم الإمكان العام واحدا يشمل الإمكان الخاص والوجوب والامتناع معا بل له مفهومان يراد منه عند إطلاقه أحدهما الصادق إما بالإمكان الخاص مع الوجوب فقط أو به","english":"The defect is what was explained earlier: general possibility does not have a single concept embracing specific possibility, necessity and impossibility together. Rather, it has two concepts. When the expression is used, one of them is intended: it applies either to specific possibility together with necessity alone, or to specific possibility—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I088.png","source_sha256":"19ba4bf09b68bcc35f8a8dcee4b8fec0a6e0de2cb93dc180539e2d485f6b0fdd","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"join_out":"I089.lower.01","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"stage81_same_editor_visual_review":"whole_visible_page_second_source_inspection_unresolved_glyphs_not_externally_certified","stage81_register_audit":"one upper line + continuous lower gloss","stage81_boundary_audit":"I:87 lower -> I:88 lower -> I:89 lower; upper I:88 -> I:89","stage81_proof_note":"Modal possibility: the negative is لأن عدمه ضروري, already corrected in Stage79. Printed contrast of broad/particular possibility preserved."},{"id":"I088.upper.01","volume":1,"page":88,"physical_pdf_page":101,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"الإمكان (بالنظر إلى الاستقبال) بمعنى جواز وجود الشيء في المستقبل","english":"—possibility “with regard to the future,” meaning that a thing may exist in the future—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I088.png","source_sha256":"19ba4bf09b68bcc35f8a8dcee4b8fec0a6e0de2cb93dc180539e2d485f6b0fdd","stage":71,"source_transcription_note":"Manual reading of the supplied scan; punctuation and word spacing lightly normalized. Parentheses in the upper register retain printed lemma boundaries; this is not a diplomatic edition.","whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"independent_review":false,"join_in":"I087.upper.01","join_out":"I089.upper.01","all_source_words_resolved":true,"first_pass_all_register_page_closed":false,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"stage81_same_editor_visual_review":"whole_visible_page_second_source_inspection_unresolved_glyphs_not_externally_certified","stage81_register_audit":"one upper line + continuous lower gloss","stage81_boundary_audit":"I:87 lower -> I:88 lower -> I:89 lower; upper I:88 -> I:89","stage81_proof_note":"Modal possibility: the negative is لأن عدمه ضروري, already corrected in Stage79. Printed contrast of broad/particular possibility preserved."},{"id":"I089.lower.01","volume":1,"page":89,"physical_pdf_page":102,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"مع الامتناع فقط لا بالسلامة معا فحينئذ لا نسلم بطلان النتيجة المذكورة لأن الإمكان العام المنفي معنى والمثبت معنى آخر فلا تناقض أصلا تمت القاعدة المذكورة فتأمله فإنه دقيق جدا اه","english":"—with impossibility alone, not with freedom from both together. In that case we do not concede that the stated conclusion is false: the general possibility being denied has one meaning, while what is affirmed has another. There is therefore no contradiction at all. This concludes the stated rule. Reflect on it, for it is exceedingly subtle. End.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I089.png","stage":70,"join_in":"I088.lower.final","all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"d3a46121b8edffbb9e9663ddd541832bedb84139861e8a9a15bf796f049a2de7","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I089.lower.02","volume":1,"page":89,"physical_pdf_page":102,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله من غير نظر إلى الماضي والحال الخ) قال في شرح المقاصد ومنهم من اشترط في الممكن الاستقبالي العدم في الحال لأن الوجود يخرجه إلى الوجوب ضرورة ⟦فجيب؟⟧ منه الخلو والتحقيق أنه ممكن في جانبي الوجود والعدم فكما أن الوجود يخرجه إلى الوجوب ويشترط الخلو عنه كذلك العدم يخرجه إلى الامتناع فيلزم اشتراط الخلو عنه فيلزم ارتفاع النقيضين بل اجتماعهما اه","english":"On “Without considering the past or present, etc.” He says in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid: “Some make present nonexistence a condition of the future-possible, because existence necessarily removes it into necessity, so freedom from existence is required. The correct account is that it is possible on both the side of existence and the side of nonexistence. Just as existence removes it into necessity, so that freedom from existence is required, nonexistence removes it into impossibility, so that freedom from that too must be required. This entails the absence of both contradictories—or rather, their conjunction.” End of quotation.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I089.png","stage":70,"readings":[{"locus":"فجيب منه الخلو","status":"The small connective before منه الخلو is indistinct; the translation gives the apparent sense “freedom from it is required,” not a certified transcription of that connective."}],"all_source_words_resolved":false,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"d3a46121b8edffbb9e9663ddd541832bedb84139861e8a9a15bf796f049a2de7","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I089.lower.03","volume":1,"page":89,"physical_pdf_page":102,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"أقول القول بوجوب الخلو عن الوجود أو العدم إما أن يراد منه وجوبه في الحال قلنا ممنوع فإن الوجوب أو الامتناع في الحال لا ينافي الإمكان في الاستقبال إذ الكلام في الوجوب والامتناع بالغير كما هو ظاهر وإما أن يراد وجوب الخلو في الاستقبال فلا يفيد اشتراط الوجود والعدم في الحال كما هو المطلوب فتأمل ثم قال والظاهر أن من اشترط ذلك أراد بالإمكان الاستقبالي إمكان حدوث الوجود وطريانه في المستقبل وهو إنما يستلزم إمكان عدم الحدوث","english":"I say: requiring freedom from existence or nonexistence must mean either requiring it in the present or requiring it in the future. On the first interpretation, we deny the requirement: present necessity or impossibility does not conflict with future possibility, since the discussion clearly concerns necessity and impossibility through another. On the second interpretation, requiring such freedom in the future does not establish a condition concerning present existence or nonexistence, which is the point at issue. Reflect. He then says: “It appears that whoever imposed that condition meant by future possibility the possibility of existence beginning and supervening in the future. This entails only the possibility of its not beginning—”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I089.png","stage":70,"join_out":"I090.lower.01","editorial":"The first-person objection belongs to the lower annotation. It is not silently attributed to Sanandaji or to Wasim. The quotation continues on I:90.","all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"d3a46121b8edffbb9e9663ddd541832bedb84139861e8a9a15bf796f049a2de7","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I089.upper.01","volume":1,"page":89,"physical_pdf_page":102,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"من غير نظر إلى الماضي والحال وذلك لأن الإمكان في مقابلة الضرورة وكلما كان الشيء أخلى من الضرورة كان أحق باسم الممكن وذلك في المستقبل إذ لا يلزم فيه حال الشيء من الوجود والعدم بخلاف الماضي والحال فإنه قد تحقق فيهما أحدهما","english":"—without considering the past or the present. Possibility is opposed to necessity, and the freer a thing is from necessity, the more properly it is called possible. This applies to the future, in which the thing's condition—existence or nonexistence—is not fixed, unlike the past and present, in which one of the two has already been realised.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I089.png","stage":70,"join_in":"I088.upper.final","all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"d3a46121b8edffbb9e9663ddd541832bedb84139861e8a9a15bf796f049a2de7","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I089.upper.02","volume":1,"page":89,"physical_pdf_page":102,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وسمي بالإمكان الاستقبالي) قد يعتبر (بمعنى تهيؤ المادة لحصول الشيء) من الصور والصفات والأعراض (باعتبار تحقق الشرائط شيئا فشيئا) ⟦⟦وإلا تنتهي؟⟧؟⟧ إلى حد الوجوب الحاصل عند تمام العلة فتتفاوت شدة وضعفا بحسب القرب من الحصول والبعد عنه كاستعداد الإنسانية الحاصل للنطفة ثم العلقة ثم المضغة وهذا الإمكان ليس لازما للماهية لأنه يوجد بعد العدم بحصول بعض الشرائط والأسباب ويعدم بعد الوجود بحصول الشيء بالفعل (فيسمى بالإمكان الاستعدادي وهذا) المعنى الأخير للإمكان (مراد من قال كل","english":"“It is called future possibility.” [Possibility] may also be considered “as the preparedness of matter for a thing to occur”—a form, quality or accident—“through the conditions being realised little by little.” [The short connective concerning reaching or not reaching] the limit of necessity obtained when the cause is complete is not securely read. It therefore varies in strength and weakness according to proximity to, or remoteness from, actual occurrence: for example, the readiness for humanity present in a drop of semen, then in a clinging embryo, then in a morsel-like embryo. This possibility is not inseparable from the quiddity. It comes to exist after not existing when some conditions and causes arise, and ceases after having existed when the thing becomes actual. “It is therefore called dispositional possibility. This”—last meaning of possibility—“is intended by whoever says: every—”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I089.png","stage":70,"join_out":"I090.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"⟦وإلا تنتهي؟⟧","status":"The connective and its syntactic force remain uncertain after enlargement. The English deliberately leaves this link unresolved; it does not assert either reading as definitive."}],"proof_notes":["Upper-register comparison confirms the surrounding distinction between gradual preparedness and completed causation but does not securely resolve the connective. Initial definite “Otherwise” removed.","Upper-register comparison confirms the surrounding distinction between gradual preparedness and completed causation but does not securely resolve the connective. Initial definite “Otherwise” removed."],"all_source_words_resolved":false,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"d3a46121b8edffbb9e9663ddd541832bedb84139861e8a9a15bf796f049a2de7","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I090.lower.01","volume":1,"page":90,"physical_pdf_page":103,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"لا إمكان حدوث العدم ليلزم اشتراط الوجود في الحال بل لو اعتبر الإمكان الاستقبالي في جانب العدم بمعنى إمكان طريان العدم بشرط الوجود في الحال صح ذلك من غير لزوم المحال اه أي من غير لزومه في الصورتين","english":"—not the possibility of nonexistence beginning, which would require existence in the present. Rather, were future possibility considered on the side of nonexistence, in the sense of nonexistence possibly supervening on the condition of present existence, that would be valid without an impossibility following.” End. That is, without an impossibility following in either case.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I090.png","stage":70,"join_in":"I089.lower.03","all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"94baccce2e0ee861d5fc439dc6118110ff6b3b92ce4a0dbc438d99c76fb63edf","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I090.lower.02","volume":1,"page":90,"physical_pdf_page":103,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"أقول فظهر مما قررنا أن حديث اشتراط الخلو في الحال ممنوع وأن الظاهر من الإمكان الاستقبالي إذا قيد بجانب الوجود هو معناه أن وجوده في الاستقبال ممكن أعم من أن يكون بطريق الحدوث والطريان أو بطريق الاستمرار وكذا في جانب العدم فتأمل","english":"I say: what we have established shows that the supposed requirement of freedom [from existence and nonexistence] in the present is denied. The apparent meaning of future possibility, when restricted to the side of existence, is that future existence is possible, whether by beginning and supervening or by continuing. The same applies on the side of nonexistence. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I090.png","stage":70,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"94baccce2e0ee861d5fc439dc6118110ff6b3b92ce4a0dbc438d99c76fb63edf","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I090.lower.03","volume":1,"page":90,"physical_pdf_page":103,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لاحتاجت إلى مادة أخرى الخ) هذا استدلال على كون المادة المفتقر إليها قديمة وأما الاستدلال على الافتقار إليها فهو متأت من قوله تكون محلا لإمكانه ومبني كلامهما على أن كل حادث بهذا المعنى ممكن كما صرح به","english":"On “It would require further matter, etc.” This argues that the matter required is pre-existent. The argument that matter is required in the first place comes from his statement that it is a subject for the thing's possibility. Both authors' statements rest on the proposition that everything originated in this sense is possible, as he explicitly states.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I090.png","stage":70,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"94baccce2e0ee861d5fc439dc6118110ff6b3b92ce4a0dbc438d99c76fb63edf","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I090.lower.04","volume":1,"page":90,"physical_pdf_page":103,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لامتناع الانقلاب) قد يقال لا نسلم كون الحادث قبل الوجود ممكنا ولامتناع الانقلاب فإن الحادث ممتنع في الأزل لأن الأزلية تنافي الحدوث ثم ينقلب ممكنا فيما لا يزال وأجيب بأن قولكم في الأزل إن كان قيدا للحادث فلا نسلم أنه يصير ممكنا فيما لا يزال إذ الحادث في الأزل ممتنع أزلا وأبدا وإن كان قيدا لممتنع فلا نسلم أن الحادث ممتنع في الأزل بل هو ممكن أزلا وأبدا فأزلية الإمكان ثابتة للحادث دائما وإمكان الأزلية ممتنع عنه دائما","english":"On “Since its modal status cannot reverse.” It may be said: we do not concede that the originated thing is possible before existence, nor that reversal is impossible. The originated is impossible in pre-eternity, because pre-eternity conflicts with origination; it then becomes possible thereafter. The answer is that, in “impossible in pre-eternity,” the qualification “in pre-eternity” qualifies either the originated thing or its impossibility. If it qualifies the originated thing, we deny that such a thing becomes possible thereafter: something originated in pre-eternity is impossible always, before and after. If it qualifies “impossible,” we deny that the originated thing is impossible in pre-eternity; rather, it is possible always, before and after. Thus the eternity of possibility always belongs to the originated thing, whereas the possibility of eternity is always excluded from it.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I090.png","stage":70,"editorial":"The printed argument distinguishes أزلية الإمكان (the pre-eternity of possibility) from إمكان الأزلية (the possibility of being pre-eternal). These are not interchangeable.","all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"94baccce2e0ee861d5fc439dc6118110ff6b3b92ce4a0dbc438d99c76fb63edf","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I090.lower.05","volume":1,"page":90,"physical_pdf_page":103,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله إذ لا معنى لقيام إمكان الشيء الخ) قد يقال إمكان الشيء هو اقتدار الفاعل عليه فيكون قائما بالفاعل فلذلك ممنوع فإن الاقتدار المذكور قد يعلل بالإمكان وعدمه بعدمه فيقال","english":"On “Since there is no sense in a thing's possibility inhering …, etc.” It may be said that a thing's possibility is the agent's power over it and thus inheres in the agent. This is denied: the power just mentioned can itself be explained by possibility, and the absence of power by its absence. Thus one says—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I090.png","stage":70,"join_out":"I091.lower.01","all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"94baccce2e0ee861d5fc439dc6118110ff6b3b92ce4a0dbc438d99c76fb63edf","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I090.upper.01","volume":1,"page":90,"physical_pdf_page":103,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"حادث مفتقر إلى مادة) قديمة إذ لو كانت حادثة لاحتاجت إلى مادة أخرى وهلم جرا فيتسلسل (تكون) تلك المادة (محلا للإمكان) فإن الحادث قبل وجوده ممكن لامتناع الانقلاب وكل ممكن فله إمكان وهو وجودي وليس بجوهر لكونه إضافيا فيكون عرضا ويستدعي محلا موجودا وليس نفس ذلك الحادث لامتناع تقدمه على نفسه ولا أمر منفصل إذ لا معنى لقيام إمكان الشيء بالأمر المنفصل عنه بل متعلقا به وهو المعنى بالمادة (و) يفتقر إلى (مدة) سابقة على وجوده (بها يكون تعاقب الحوادث) على المادة من الشرائط والأسباب (وإنما يتم","english":"“Originated thing requires matter”—pre-existent matter; for if that matter were itself originated, it would require further matter, and so on without end. “[That matter] is a subject for possibility.” Before coming to exist, the originated thing is possible, since its modal status cannot reverse. Everything possible has a possibility; this is an existent item, but not a substance, since it is relational. It is therefore an accident requiring an existent subject. That subject is not the originated thing itself, since it cannot precede itself, nor a separate thing, since there is no sense in a thing's possibility inhering in something separate from it. Rather, it is something connected with it; that is what is meant by matter. “And” it requires “a duration” preceding its existence, “through which originated occurrences succeed one another” as conditions and causes occur in matter. “Their statement is valid only—”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I090.png","stage":70,"join_in":"I089.upper.02","join_out":"I091.upper.01","proof_notes":["Native embedded source image, complete upper register: تقدمه على نفسه, تعاقب الحوادث, and (وإنما يتم) are legible. These correct the initial transcription, not the printed witness.","Native embedded source image, complete upper register: تقدمه على نفسه, تعاقب الحوادث, and (وإنما يتم) are legible. These correct the initial transcription, not the printed witness."],"all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"94baccce2e0ee861d5fc439dc6118110ff6b3b92ce4a0dbc438d99c76fb63edf","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I091.lower.01","volume":1,"page":91,"physical_pdf_page":104,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"هذا مقدور لكونه ممكنا وذاك غير مقدور لكونه ممتنعا فافهم","english":"“This falls within power because it is possible, and that does not fall within power because it is impossible.” Understand this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I091.png","stage":70,"join_in":"I090.lower.05","all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"de1313831741ea383fc1cb34130eb53e80428b6aa7d434a03e894c332879428c","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I091.lower.02","volume":1,"page":91,"physical_pdf_page":104,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وإلا لم يكن الممكن حال عدمه الخ) فيه أنه لا يستلزم المدعى فإن الخصم يعترف بأن الممكن له الإمكان قبل وجوده كما سبق وأنه لازم للماهية مع أنه قائل باقتضائه المحل الموجود الذي هو مادة الممكن فليس لزومه للماهية منافيا لاقتضائه المحل الموجود كما هو ظاهر فالصواب أن يتمسك في عدم الاقتضاء المذكور بكون الإمكان أمرا اعتباريا غير مقتض لمحل موجود ليكون ردا على مدعى الخصم وما تمسك به من كون الإمكان أمرا وجوديا مقتضيا لمحل موجود فتفحص","english":"On “Otherwise the contingent would not [have it] in its state of nonexistence, etc.” The difficulty is that this does not establish the claim. The opponent concedes, as above, that the contingent has possibility before its existence and that this is inseparable from its quiddity, while also holding that it requires an existent subject, namely the contingent thing's matter. Its inseparability from the quiddity therefore does not, evidently, conflict with requiring an existent subject. The correct way to argue that it does not require such a subject is to maintain that possibility is a matter of consideration, not something requiring an existent subject. This would answer both the opponent's claim and the premise used to support it: that possibility is an existent item requiring an existent subject. Examine this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I091.png","stage":70,"readings":[{"locus":"وإلا لم يكن الممكن حال عدمه","status":"The opening is now read as a counterfactual lemma. The main-text word sequence is not wholly resolved; this parallel internal quotation is supporting context, not an independent witness."}],"proof_notes":["Internal lemma compared with the restored counterfactual at I:91 upper; the objection remains separately assigned to the lower annotation.","Internal lemma compared with the restored counterfactual at I:91 upper; the objection remains separately assigned to the lower annotation."],"all_source_words_resolved":false,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"de1313831741ea383fc1cb34130eb53e80428b6aa7d434a03e894c332879428c","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I091.lower.03","volume":1,"page":91,"physical_pdf_page":104,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ويلزمه الحكم الخ) قال في شرح المقاصد ولتلازم هاتين المعنيين بل تقارب مفهوميهما جدا قد يجعل الثاني تفسيرا للأول اه فافهم","english":"On “And, as a consequence, the judgement …, etc.” He says in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid: “Because these two meanings entail one another—indeed, because their concepts are extremely close—the second is sometimes made an explanation of the first.” End. Understand this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I091.png","stage":70,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"de1313831741ea383fc1cb34130eb53e80428b6aa7d434a03e894c332879428c","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I091.lower.04","volume":1,"page":91,"physical_pdf_page":104,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله اللذين هما متساويان لا ترجح لأحدهما الخ) اعلم أن المستحيل الضروري هو ترجح أحد طرفي الممكن أي وجوده وعدمه على الآخر من غير مرجح أعني مؤثر خارج من نفس ذلك الممكن يؤثر فيه بتحصيل أحد طرفيه وترجيحه على طرفه الآخر ⟦⟦وتقديمه على ذلك؟⟧؟⟧ فإنه لو لم يكن أحد الطرفين من مؤثر لكان من نفسه فكان واجبا أو ممتنعا هذا خلف وأنه لو لم يكن المؤثر خارجا بل كان من نفس الممكن لم يكن نسبته إلى طرفيه على السواء وقد قرر أنه كذلك هذا خلف فظهر أن ترجح أحد طرفي الممكن من نفسه وكذا ترجيح الممكن لأحد طرفيه بنفسه ممتنعان ضرورة وهما متلازمان ولذا تراهم قد يذكرون كلا مكان الآخر بقي أن ترجيح المؤثر الخارج عن الممكن لأحد طرفيه هل","english":"On “The alternatives are equal, neither being preponderant, etc.” Know that the self-evident impossibility is one of the contingent thing's two alternatives—its existence or nonexistence—becoming preponderant over the other without a preponderating factor: an efficient cause external to that contingent thing, which acts on it by bringing about one alternative and making it preponderant over the other. [The short connective here is difficult.] If neither alternative were due to an efficient cause, it would be due to the thing itself, making the thing necessary or impossible: a contradiction. And if the efficient cause were not external but belonged to the contingent thing itself, the thing's relation to the two alternatives would not be equal; yet it has been established that it is equal: again a contradiction. It follows that an alternative becoming preponderant from the contingent thing itself, and the contingent thing itself making one of its alternatives preponderant, are both self-evidently impossible. They entail one another; hence the authors sometimes use either expression in place of the other. There remains the question whether an external efficient cause can make one contingent alternative preponderant—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I091.png","stage":70,"join_out":"I092.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"وتقديمه على ذلك","status":"The short transition before فإنه لو لم يكن is not securely resolved; its location is preserved, rather than silently replacing it with an inferred argument."}],"all_source_words_resolved":false,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"de1313831741ea383fc1cb34130eb53e80428b6aa7d434a03e894c332879428c","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I091.upper.01","volume":1,"page":91,"physical_pdf_page":104,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"قولهم بهذا الافتقار (لو سلم أن كل حادث بهذا المعنى) يعني بالإمكان الاستعدادي (ممكن) لكنه غير مسلم وليس هذا ⟦في شيء؟⟧ من الانقلاب لأن المقابل للوجوب والامتناع هو الإمكان الذاتي اللازم للماهية الممكنة ونحن لا نمنعه عن الحادث وهو لا يقتضي محلا موجودا وإلا لم يكن للممكن حال عدمه ⟦حروف غير محققة⟧ لكن لازمه للماهية الممكن","english":"—their statement concerning this dependence [on matter and duration] “if it is conceded that every originated thing is possible in this sense,” namely, in the sense of dispositional possibility. But this is not conceded, and [the point has no bearing on modal reversal: the precise short expression is uncertain]. What is opposed to necessity and impossibility is intrinsic possibility, inseparable from a contingent quiddity. We do not deny this of the originated thing, but it does not require an existent subject. Otherwise the contingent would not possess [possibility] in its state of nonexistence; [the following short sequence is not securely read], while possibility is inseparable from the contingent quiddity.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I091.png","stage":70,"join_in":"I090.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"⟦في شيء؟⟧ من الانقلاب","status":"Native-image inspection suggests في شيء, replacing the initial erroneous ذاتيا; kept tentative."},{"locus":"وإلا لم يكن للممكن حال عدمه ... لكن لازمه للماهية الممكن","status":"Native-image inspection restores the counterfactual وإلا لم يكن, which the initial English had replaced by a concession. A short sequence after عدمه and the exact connective before لزومه remain unsettled. The English marks the gap rather than claiming full closure."}],"proof_notes":["Native upper image rechecked. The counterfactual construction is restored; two short readings remain explicitly unresolved.","Native upper image rechecked. The counterfactual construction is restored; two short readings remain explicitly unresolved."],"all_source_words_resolved":false,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"de1313831741ea383fc1cb34130eb53e80428b6aa7d434a03e894c332879428c","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I091.upper.02","volume":1,"page":91,"physical_pdf_page":104,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ثم) الحكم بـ (احتياج الممكن) في وجوده وعدمه (إلى المؤثر) ويلزمه الحكم (بامتناع ترجيح أحد طرفيه) اللذين هما متساويان لا ترجح لأحدهما على الآخر (بلا مرجح) من خارج (ضروري) بعد","english":"“Then,” the judgement that “the contingent needs an efficient cause” for its existence and nonexistence—and, as a consequence, the judgement that “one of its two alternatives cannot be made preponderant without a preponderating factor” external to it, the alternatives being equal with neither preponderant over the other—“is self-evident” after—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I091.png","stage":70,"join_out":"I092.upper.01","all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"de1313831741ea383fc1cb34130eb53e80428b6aa7d434a03e894c332879428c","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I092.lower.01","volume":1,"page":92,"physical_pdf_page":105,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"يجوز بلا داع ومخصص يستدعي تأثيره في أحد الطرفين أولا فالتحقيق أن ذلك المؤثر إن كان مختارا كما هو عندنا فترجيحه بلا داع كذلك جائز بل واقع ضرورة أن إرادته كافية في ذلك التخصيص كالهارب يسلك أحد الطريقين والجائع يأكل أحد الرغيفين وإن كان موجبا كما هو عند الفلاسفة فلا بد في ذلك من داع مرجح ومخصص أحد الطرفين بالوقوع كما يقولون بالدواعي المنتهية إلى الحركات والأوضاع الفلكية وأما حديث رجحان أحد طرفي الممكن من نفسه لا إلى حد الوجوب فسيأتي تحقيقه هذا غاية تحرير المقام فاحفظه","english":"—without a motive or specifying factor requiring its action on one alternative, or not? The correct account is this: if that efficient cause is a choosing agent, as we hold, such preference without a motive is possible and indeed actually occurs. Its will is sufficient for that specification: for example, a fugitive takes one of two paths, and a hungry person eats one of two loaves. If the cause is necessitating, as the philosophers hold, there must be a motive that makes one alternative preponderant and specifies its occurrence—as in their account of motives ultimately reaching celestial motions and configurations. As for one contingent alternative becoming preponderant from the thing itself without reaching necessity, its proper investigation will come later. This is the fullest clarification of the issue; retain it.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I092.png","stage":70,"join_in":"I091.lower.04","all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"8b02bb823a4739caa1a1cdf6209bd5c40e5f9a24b892fc1c41d91cb8dc19d346","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I092.lower.02","volume":1,"page":92,"physical_pdf_page":105,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله من غير افتقار إلى دليل الخ) قد يقال ذلك كسبي واستدل عليه بوجهين الأول أن الإمكان يستلزم تساوي الوجود والعدم بالنسبة إلى ذات الممكن ووقوع أحدهما بلا مرجح يستلزم رجحانه وهما متنافيان الثاني أن الممكن ما لم يرجح لم يوجد وترجيحه أمر حدث بعد أن لم يكن فيكون وجوديا ولا بد من محل وليس هو الأثر لتأخره عن الترجيح فيكون هو المؤثر فلا بد منه كذا في شرح المقاصد","english":"On “Without needing an argument, etc.” It has been said that this is acquired knowledge, and two arguments have been offered. First: possibility entails that existence and nonexistence are equal relative to the contingent thing's essence. Either alternative occurring without a preponderating factor entails its preponderance, and these two conditions conflict. Second: the contingent thing does not exist until it is made preponderant. Its being made preponderant is something that begins after not existing, so it must be an existent item requiring a subject. That subject is not the effect, since the effect is subsequent to the preponderation. It must therefore be the efficient cause, which consequently must exist. This is how the argument is presented in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I092.png","stage":70,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"8b02bb823a4739caa1a1cdf6209bd5c40e5f9a24b892fc1c41d91cb8dc19d346","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I092.lower.03","volume":1,"page":92,"physical_pdf_page":105,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(وأقول) الوجهان المذكوران لو تمت إفادتهما يدلان على تحقق امتناع الترجيح المذكور لا على كونه كسبيا لجواز أن يكونا تنبيهين لا دليلين وعلى تقدير كونهما استدلالين فالجواب على ما في شرح المقاصد هو أنهما مردودان أما الأول فلأن التساوي بالنظر إلى الذات إنما ينافي الرجحان بحسب الذات وهو غير لازم فإن قيل الترجيح إذا لم يكن بالغير كان بالذات إذ لا ثالث قلنا هذا نفس المتنازع لجواز أن يكون بحسب الاتفاق من غير سبب","english":"I say: even if the two arguments were effective, they would establish that the aforementioned preponderation is impossible, not that knowledge of its impossibility is acquired. They could be reminders directing attention to something already self-evident rather than demonstrative proofs. Even supposing they are arguments, the answer given in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid is that both are rejected. As to the first: equality relative to the essence conflicts only with preponderance through the essence; the latter does not follow. If one says that preponderation not due to another must be due to the essence, there being no third alternative, we reply that this is precisely the disputed point: it might occur by chance, without a cause.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I092.png","stage":70,"all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"8b02bb823a4739caa1a1cdf6209bd5c40e5f9a24b892fc1c41d91cb8dc19d346","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I092.lower.04","volume":1,"page":92,"physical_pdf_page":105,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(وأقول) هذا لا ينافي ما يأتي عن الشارح رحمه الله قريبا من ظهور بطلانه بأدنى التفات لأن المقصود فيما هنا المنع وهو يكفيه الاحتمال فافهم وأما الثاني فإن الترجيح مع","english":"I say: this does not conflict with the commentator's forthcoming statement—may God have mercy on him—that its falsity becomes evident with the slightest attention. What is intended here is a denial [of the inferential step], for which merely admitting an alternative possibility suffices. Understand this. As to the second argument: the preponderation accompanies—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I092.png","stage":70,"join_out":"I093.lower.01","editorial":"The English distinguishes a dialectical denial of an inference from adoption of chance as the annotator’s settled account.","all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"8b02bb823a4739caa1a1cdf6209bd5c40e5f9a24b892fc1c41d91cb8dc19d346","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I092.upper.01","volume":1,"page":92,"physical_pdf_page":105,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"تصور طرفي الحكم من غير افتقار إلى دليل فإن معنى الممكن ما لا يقتضي ذاته وجوده ولا عدمه ومعنى الاحتياج أن كلا من وجوده وعدمه لا يكون لذاته بل لأمر خارج فإنه مسلم لكن أن يقال وقوع الممكن لا لذاته ولا لأمر خارج بل بمجرد الاتفاق قلنا بطلانه يظهر بأدنى التفات ولهذا يحكم به من لا يتأتى منه النظر (وهذا) الترجيح المذكور الذي قلنا بامتناعه (غير","english":"—conceiving the two terms of the judgement, without needing an argument. “Contingent” means that whose essence entails neither its existence nor its nonexistence. “Need” means that neither its existence nor its nonexistence is due to its essence, but to something external. This is conceded. Yet one might say that the contingent occurs neither because of itself nor because of anything external, but merely by chance. We reply that the falsity of this becomes apparent with the slightest attention. Accordingly, even someone incapable of inferential reflection judges it false. “This”—aforementioned preponderation, which we said was impossible—“is different from—”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I092.png","stage":70,"join_in":"I091.upper.02","join_out":"I093.upper.01","editorial":"The printed abbreviations are expanded in the Arabic reading only where context supports مسلم. The final غير joins directly to I:93 upper text, not to the marginal stream.","all_source_words_resolved":true,"whole_page_second_pass_verified":false,"source_transcription_note":"Manual visual reading with light spacing/punctuation normalization; uncertainty brackets are editorial. Not a diplomatic or critically established text.","source_sha256":"8b02bb823a4739caa1a1cdf6209bd5c40e5f9a24b892fc1c41d91cb8dc19d346","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I093.lower.01","volume":1,"page":93,"physical_pdf_page":106,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"الوجود لا قبله ولو سلم فقيام مرجح أحد طرفي الممكن بالمؤثر دوري البطلان (أقول) إنما يتم لو أريد من الترجيح الترجيح الذي هو صفة الأثر أما لو أريد منه ما هو صفة المؤثر فهو قائم به ضرورة فالأولى في الجواب منع كونه أمرا محققا مفتقرا إلى ما يقوم به في الخارج بل هو عقلي قائم بالتصور من الممكن هذا الحكم بحدوثه","english":"—existence, not before it. Even if this is granted, the preponderator of one contingent alternative inhering in the efficient cause would involve a vicious circle. I say: that works only if “preponderance” means a property of the effect. If it means a property of the efficient cause, it necessarily belongs to that cause. The better answer is therefore to deny that it is an externally realised entity requiring an external subject. Rather, it is intellectual, belonging to the conception [of the contingent; the final judgement-clause is insecure].","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I093.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I092.lower.final","readings":[{"locus":"دوري البطلان / قائم بالتصور من الممكن هذا الحكم بحدوثه","status":"The final printed clause and the expression before “invalidity” require a clearer reading."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I093.lower.02","volume":1,"page":93,"physical_pdf_page":106,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فإن قيل لا يخلو إما أن يكون إلخ) إشارة إلى شبهة المنكرين لامتناع الممكن بلا سبب مؤثر كديمقراطيس وأتباعه القائلين بأن وجود السموات بطريق الاتفاق كما ذكره بعضهم","english":"On “If it is said: it must be either …, etc.” This points to the doubt of those who deny that the contingent cannot occur without an efficient cause, such as Democritus and his followers, who are reported to have held that the heavens exist by chance.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I093.jpg","stage":69,"editorial":"This is the historical attribution made by the printed marginal writer, not an independently verified statement about Democritus.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I093.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":93,"physical_pdf_page":106,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بل بمحض الإرادة إلخ) وفيه نظر لأن للإرادة إما أن تكون نسبتها إلى الطرفين على السواء أولا فعلى الأول لا بد لها من مرجح وعلى الثاني يلزم الإيجاب فلا بد أن تكون إلى الفعل داع باعث للفاعل عليه وحديث مساواة الطريقين أو الرغيفين من كل وجه ممنوع اه منه","english":"On “Through sheer will, etc.” This is questionable. The will’s relation to the two alternatives is either equal or unequal. If equal, it needs a preponderator; if unequal, necessitation follows. There must therefore be a motive prompting the agent to act. The claim that the paths or loaves are equal in every respect is denied. End; “from him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I093.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I093.upper.01","volume":1,"page":93,"physical_pdf_page":106,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ترجيح المختار أحد المتساويين على الآخر بلا مخصص وداع إليه بل (بمحض الإرادة) التي شأنها الترجيح والتخصيص (كالهارب) من السبع (يسلك أحد الطريقين) المتساويين في الإفضاء إلى المطلوب الذي هو النجاة (و) كـ (الجائع) يأكل أحد الرغيفين المتساويين فنحن لا نقول بامتناعه فضلا عن كونه ضروريا ولا يلزم من جوازه جواز الترجيح الذي قلنا بامتناعه إذ الفعل ههنا مترجح على الترك فإن الهارب بالإرادة يرجح سلوك أحد الطريقين على تركه كسلوك الآخر غاية الأمر أنه لا يكون إلى الفعل داع باعث للفاعل عليه (فإن قيل) لا يخلو إما أن يكون (التأثير حال الوجود) أو العدم لكن التأثير حال الوجود (تحصيل الحاصل) وإيجاد الموجود وذلك باطل (و) كذلك في (حال العدم) لأنه (جمع","english":"—a choosing agent’s preferring one of two equals without a specifying factor or motive, but “through sheer will,” whose function is to prefer and specify—as when “someone fleeing” a beast “takes one of two paths” equally suited to the desired escape, “or a hungry person” eats one of two equal loaves. We do not hold this impossible, much less self-evidently impossible. Its possibility does not entail the possibility of the preponderance we declared impossible: here acting is preferred to refraining. By willing, the fugitive prefers taking one path to not taking it, just as with the other path. At most, there is no motive prompting the agent to this particular act. “If it is said”: efficient action must occur either “while existence obtains” or while nonexistence obtains. Yet efficient action during existence is “producing what is already produced,” bringing into being what already exists, which is invalid. “And” the same applies “during nonexistence,” because it “combines—”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I093.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I092.upper.final","join_out":"I094.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false}]);